1
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:07,400
So today we're going to talk about data.

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:10,880
And where we are is that you know how

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,720
to train a model, given data.

4
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:17,880
Last time we saw evaluations, so we know what a good model is.

5
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:20,340
And for the next two lectures, this week

6
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:24,760
we're going to focus on what data should we train on.

7
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:27,480
And I want to argue that data is the most important thing

8
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,880
to get right in language models.

9
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,720
And one justification for this is

10
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:35,020
if you look at what companies actually disclose--

11
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:38,120
So here is the Llama 3 paper.

12
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:41,540
They have full transparency into the architecture--

13
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:44,440
well of course, because it's open-weight model--

14
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,600
they even tell you about their training procedures,

15
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,120
but they don't say anything about their data.

16
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:56,400
So they just say, we train for a variety of data sources

17
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,560
and do some stuff.

18
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:02,200
And there's good reasons for this secrecy.

19
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:07,360
One is that data is your competitive secret sauce.

20
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:08,260
Competitive dynamics.

21
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:11,160
You don't want your competitors to know what you're doing.

22
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:13,040
And the second thing, which we'll

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,258
talk a little bit more about, is copyright liability.

24
00:01:16,258 --> 00:01:18,800
You don't want to get sued if you tell people you're training

25
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,600
on certain types of data.

26
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:25,480
So data is a long topic in machine learning.

27
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,520
And before foundation models, data work

28
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:33,880
meant you annotate-- label data for supervised learning.

29
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:37,380
Nowadays there's less manual annotation--

30
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,560
at least in pre-training-- but there's still

31
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,600
a lot of curation and cleaning work that needs to be done.

32
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,500
And so fundamentally, this hasn't really changed.

33
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,960
Data has always been a bottleneck,

34
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:51,120
and always will be a bottleneck because it's

35
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,960
in some sense a long-tail problem,

36
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,620
and that scales with human effort.

37
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:59,370
So if you have a lot of people trying to work on the problem,

38
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:01,170
well, there's only so many people

39
00:02:01,170 --> 00:02:03,670
that can work on architectures and systems.

40
00:02:03,670 --> 00:02:06,676
But data, well, that's-- especially if you're training

41
00:02:06,676 --> 00:02:09,009
a foundation model that's trying to do all these things,

42
00:02:09,009 --> 00:02:10,910
you can paralyze that effort easily.

43
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:15,130
So that's why these data teams and these model developers

44
00:02:15,130 --> 00:02:18,410
are actually quite big.

45
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:23,850
So data comes in at different stages of the pipeline.

46
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:26,530
Today we're focusing mostly on pre-training.

47
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:28,350
So pre-training, you take raw data—

48
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:30,370
these are documents from the web.

49
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:32,570
Then you move into mid-training where

50
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:34,650
you train on more high quality data

51
00:02:34,650 --> 00:02:38,010
to enhance certain capabilities, and give long context, things

52
00:02:38,010 --> 00:02:38,588
like that.

53
00:02:38,588 --> 00:02:40,130
And then finally post-training, where

54
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,410
you're training on things like chat transcripts,

55
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:45,910
or if you're doing reinforcement learning,

56
00:02:45,910 --> 00:02:48,210
you get some environments that you're training on.

57
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,970
This becomes more task specific.

58
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:55,730
In practice the lines are a bit blurry,

59
00:02:55,730 --> 00:02:59,770
and there could be more than three stages.

60
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:02,810
But this is a basic template.

61
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:05,270
The trend is that we go from training

62
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:06,770
on large amounts of low quality data

63
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:10,770
to smaller amounts of high quality data.

64
00:03:10,770 --> 00:03:13,610
So as a result of this process—

65
00:03:13,610 --> 00:03:16,050
you'll see in the literature when we talk about the base

66
00:03:16,050 --> 00:03:19,490
model, this usually means after pre-training and mid-training,

67
00:03:19,490 --> 00:03:24,330
instruct models or chat models, or post-training.

68
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:26,490
But of course, the lines are becoming

69
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:28,770
blurry enough that what is the base model anymore

70
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:30,790
is really unclear.

71
00:03:30,790 --> 00:03:36,070
And more recently, the largest models, there's no base model.

72
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:39,510
There's just Qwen3.5-397B-A17B, and that's it.

73
00:03:39,510 --> 00:03:44,010
You don't see the intermediate checkpoints.

74
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:48,550
So for models that are open source like OLMo from AI2,

75
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:51,590
you can see everything that's happening.

76
00:03:51,590 --> 00:03:54,690
And so, we'll study some of these models.

77
00:03:54,690 --> 00:03:58,570
So for example, in pre-training, there's a bunch of sources which

78
00:03:58,570 --> 00:04:01,050
we'll talk about what these mean, but—

79
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:06,050
web pages, academic papers, math pages, proofs, and so on.

80
00:04:06,050 --> 00:04:09,510
There's mid-training—higher quality web data,

81
00:04:09,510 --> 00:04:14,410
instruction data，bunch of synthetic data often goes here.

82
00:04:14,410 --> 00:04:19,990
And then finally post-training，there is a bunch of chat logs.

83
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:21,410
There are more math and reasoning，

84
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:27,170
and coding，you do safety at this point as well.

85
00:04:27,170 --> 00:04:30,010
So what are all these datasets?

86
00:04:30,010 --> 00:04:33,090
How do you choose them，and how do you process them?

87
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:36,730
So that's the main question.

88
00:04:36,730 --> 00:04:40,510
So in the spirit of the class，we're going to talk about data.

89
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:42,510
I'm going to start from scratch.

90
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:45,410
So where does data come from?

91
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:50,210
So one might hear in the hallway，

92
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:53,300
oh，language models are trained on the entire internet.

93
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,540
So first of all，this doesn't really quite type-check.

94
00:04:56,540 --> 00:04:58,380
Because for that to be true，it would

95
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:01,300
have to be an agent that-- like an RL agent that goes

96
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:02,920
on the internet and does stuff.

97
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,620
But that's not actually how pre-training works.

98
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:09,700
Slightly more accurately，it's trained on the public world

99
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:14,180
wide web，but this is not quite accurate for reasons

100
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,060
I'll explain.

101
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:21,420
OK，so first of all，the web is actually a bunch of live servers

102
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:24,040
that just exist in the world，and you can connect to.

103
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:27,740
So take any website-- you can go and download the page.

104
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:31,420
Or，you can basically send a request and get back a response.

105
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:34,100
So you can't actually train on these live servers

106
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:36,620
unless you're training an RL agent.

107
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:40,060
So typically what you do is you have a crawler，

108
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:43,620
or someone builds a crawler，not necessarily you.

109
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:47,280
The crawler needs to discover what web pages are out there，

110
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,700
starting with the seed set，and it downloads the discovered web

111
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:52,480
pages as it is crawling.

112
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:59,660
But still，can't just run a crawl

113
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:01,980
and download all the web pages on the internet，

114
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,260
and there's a few reasons for this.

115
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:09,480
One is that a lot of the web content is actually dynamic.

116
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:12,460
So especially these days，a lot of sites are apps.

117
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:15,220
The URL isn't even as full of specification

118
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:20,180
of the content，it's just an app that you interact with，

119
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,060
and you need to often click buttons or submit forms

120
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:25,140
to access content.

121
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,520
For example, if you're on Discord or something,

122
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:32,900
it's not like you can just crawl Discord and get all the content

123
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:34,860
on Discord.

124
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:38,060
So that's one kind of thing, and a lot of content

125
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:42,580
is actually known as in the deep web, which is not just

126
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:46,860
doing web page crawling and following

127
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:50,990
hyperlinks, which is a very traditional model of web

128
00:06:50,990 --> 00:06:52,550
crawling.

129
00:06:52,550 --> 00:06:56,450
The second thing is that authentication.

130
00:06:56,450 --> 00:07:00,510
So some pages need a login, an account, and generally

131
00:07:00,510 --> 00:07:03,550
you have to pay.

132
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:08,150
For example, there's Facebook, X, LinkedIn, New York Times.

133
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:10,030
There's actually huge amounts of content

134
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:12,690
that are actually locked up behind these walled gardens.

135
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:15,950
So these are technically on the internet or on the web,

136
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:21,350
but you can't just build a crawler and go crawl Facebook.

137
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:23,383
I guess if you're Facebook, you can crawl--

138
00:07:23,383 --> 00:07:25,550
you don't need to crawl Facebook, you have the data,

139
00:07:25,550 --> 00:07:26,633
and you can train a model.

140
00:07:26,633 --> 00:07:30,690
Or if you're X AI, you can train on X. But if you're anyone else,

141
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:34,830
you can't actually access this content.

142
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:38,610
OK, suppose you don't have the authentication issue.

143
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:43,210
There's still potential restrictions here.

144
00:07:43,210 --> 00:07:46,350
So there's something called robots.txt,

145
00:07:46,350 --> 00:07:49,070
and this is a file that is usually

146
00:07:49,070 --> 00:07:54,290
placed at the root level of a directory, such as nytimes.com,

147
00:07:54,290 --> 00:07:55,910
and it basically tells you what you're

148
00:07:55,910 --> 00:07:57,670
allowed to crawl and whatnot.

149
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:00,250
So these things are-- a lot of things are disallowed,

150
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:06,030
such as OAI-SearchBot, or PerplexityBot, QuoraBot.

151
00:08:06,030 --> 00:08:12,030
I think basically, ChatGPT-User, ClaudeBot, and all these things.

152
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:17,350
OK, so this is not a tech--

153
00:08:17,350 --> 00:08:21,550
this is not a legal restriction, this just like, you're supposed to be a good citizen

154
00:08:21,550 --> 00:08:23,490
and look at robots.txt.

155
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,550
And if your name is on that list,

156
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:26,970
then you should not crawl it.

157
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:31,150
That's basically the-- not even a contract,

158
00:08:31,150 --> 00:08:36,470
that is the thing you're supposed to do.

159
00:08:36,470 --> 00:08:40,630
A lot of websites now use something like Cloudflare

160
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:43,470
to detect and block bot activity.

161
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:45,290
So sometimes you've gone to a website,

162
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:46,930
and it gives you a CAPTCHA.

163
00:08:46,930 --> 00:08:49,230
That's because somehow, some algorithm

164
00:08:49,230 --> 00:08:51,150
has detected that you might be a bot,

165
00:08:51,150 --> 00:08:53,110
and then it's making sure you're human.

166
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:56,942
And if you're actually a bot, then, well, you can't--

167
00:08:56,942 --> 00:08:58,650
I mean, you could try to get around this,

168
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:01,950
but generally this is an obstacle in your way.

169
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:06,590
A website might block certain IP addresses or countries,

170
00:09:06,590 --> 00:09:08,370
and they might have rate limits.

171
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:09,750
So there's technical restrictions

172
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:15,030
on what you can actually crawl, and then there's

173
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:17,350
also legal restrictions.

174
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:22,070
So websites have terms of service

175
00:09:22,070 --> 00:09:23,510
that say, if you go to a website,

176
00:09:23,510 --> 00:09:26,830
you have to obey this contract to use the website.

177
00:09:26,830 --> 00:09:30,090
And the terms of service often will say, if you're a bot,

178
00:09:30,090 --> 00:09:30,910
go away.

179
00:09:30,910 --> 00:09:34,230
You cannot use this, the content of the site for AI training,

180
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:37,030
or whatever it might be.

181
00:09:37,030 --> 00:09:42,870
And also, even if terms of service don't say anything,

182
00:09:42,870 --> 00:09:47,760
the content of the website might have a license,

183
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,460
or you might not have a license to train.

184
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:52,760
And we'll talk more about this in a bit.

185
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:54,400
So there's legal restrictions on what

186
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,160
you are allowed to train on.

187
00:09:57,160 --> 00:10:00,360
These things are also evolving over time.

188
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:03,400
So there's a nice paper by Shane Longpre

189
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:07,120
called Consent in Crisis, and what

190
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:09,380
they did is they examined the restrictions--

191
00:10:09,380 --> 00:10:11,760
both the technical restrictions, robots.txt,

192
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:14,800
and the legal restrictions, the terms of service,

193
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:17,100
for various URLs in common datasets,

194
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:19,240
which we'll talk about later.

195
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:21,600
And the conclusion was that the restrictions

196
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:22,900
have increased over time.

197
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:28,160
So here's a graphic that shows-- the top one shows robots.txt.

198
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:29,920
And if you just look at this red,

199
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:32,840
that's the full restrictions.

200
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:39,480
And up until 2023, everything was fairly constant.

201
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:43,680
And then up until mid 2023, all of a sudden

202
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:46,560
you see the amount of the fraction of websites

203
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:51,920
that have full restrictions on it has grown to almost 50% here.

204
00:10:51,920 --> 00:11:00,000
And for terms, there's a similar trend where I guess in 2016,

205
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,460
no one really put any terms on their pages.

206
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:07,920
And now most pages put some terms, and most of the terms

207
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:12,800
say you can't use this for AI.

208
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:18,560
So even though it was possible to crawl the internet back in,

209
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:22,000
let's say 2020, the internet actually,

210
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,260
at least what you can legally crawl, is actually much smaller.

211
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:34,060
So of course, these are guidelines.

212
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:36,360
And sometimes, either intentionally

213
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:40,140
or not intentionally, you could violate these things.

214
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:44,640
So this is-- sometimes you get into these situations where

215
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:48,960
there's this guy who--what was this?

216
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,320
I forget the name of this site--

217
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:54,480
was complaining about Anthropic crawling them,

218
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,080
hitting their servers a million times

219
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:59,960
in 24 hours, which was not good.

220
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:02,100
And then Read the Docs was also getting hammered.

221
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:04,280
So there was a period where, which I think probably

222
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:07,560
has hopefully been corrected by now, that crawlers

223
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:12,420
were hammering websites.

224
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:16,840
And there the-- here, this is interesting,

225
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:19,560
is like even before talking about copyright issues,

226
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:24,120
there's, I think, problems with crawling either violating

227
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:31,600
terms of service, or robots.txt, or just low up-down,

228
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:34,280
generating a lot of server load, which this costs money

229
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:37,080
for the person hosting it and also degrades service

230
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:39,520
for everyone else trying to use it.

231
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:42,885
So there are issues with crawling.

232
00:12:42,885 --> 00:12:44,510
And then, of course, there's copyright,

233
00:12:44,510 --> 00:12:46,830
which we'll talk about soon.

234
00:12:46,830 --> 00:12:50,970
That's a whole other complex can of worms.

235
00:12:50,970 --> 00:12:55,090
And then finally, there's these things called shadow libraries,

236
00:12:55,090 --> 00:12:58,010
which are also part of the web.

237
00:12:58,010 --> 00:13:03,010
So examples are LibGen, or Anna's Archive.

238
00:13:03,010 --> 00:13:07,150
And these completely disregard copyright and bypass paywalls.

239
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:11,690
So someone has collected a ton of data-- usually

240
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:14,190
books and articles that are all copyrighted,

241
00:13:14,190 --> 00:13:16,130
and people have to pay for these--

242
00:13:16,130 --> 00:13:20,530
and have made them available for free.

243
00:13:20,530 --> 00:13:23,910
And there have been a lot of takedown orders, lawsuits,

244
00:13:23,910 --> 00:13:26,410
and these are blocked, but usually these

245
00:13:26,410 --> 00:13:30,090
are circumvented because they just make

246
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:32,490
servers in other countries.

247
00:13:32,490 --> 00:13:37,010
And the people doing this argue that this is making free

248
00:13:37,010 --> 00:13:39,850
what's freely available, what should have been freed,

249
00:13:39,850 --> 00:13:42,290
but generally from a legal perspective,

250
00:13:42,290 --> 00:13:45,270
this is piracy and copyright infringement.

251
00:13:45,270 --> 00:13:47,390
And we'll come back to this point later.

252
00:13:47,390 --> 00:13:52,530
So there are a lot of books and papers on these shadow

253
00:13:52,530 --> 00:13:54,850
libraries.

254
00:13:54,850 --> 00:13:57,850
So the summary so far, the internet

255
00:13:57,850 --> 00:14:03,010
is a huge and messy and scary place,

256
00:14:03,010 --> 00:14:06,850
and you can't actually get all of it.

257
00:14:06,850 --> 00:14:08,510
There are technical restrictions,

258
00:14:08,510 --> 00:14:12,730
there's legal restrictions on what data one can access.

259
00:14:12,730 --> 00:14:16,070
So now next time someone says, I trained on the internet,

260
00:14:16,070 --> 00:14:20,090
you can tell them all these things you learned.

261
00:14:20,090 --> 00:14:28,450
So now let's go to the second part of what data can we use,

262
00:14:28,450 --> 00:14:31,370
and this has to do with copyright.

263
00:14:31,370 --> 00:14:35,610
So suppose you were able to obey the terms of service,

264
00:14:35,610 --> 00:14:38,610
and you are really a good citizen,

265
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:41,620
and you obey the rate limits.

266
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:43,380
Now, there's still a question of are you

267
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:46,500
allowed to train on this data?

268
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:50,580
And this is a ongoing question that

269
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:53,260
has not been fully resolved, but there

270
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:57,020
has been a bunch of developments in the last year.

271
00:14:57,020 --> 00:15:00,260
So the legal context for thinking about this question

272
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:03,260
falls into intellectual property law,

273
00:15:03,260 --> 00:15:08,500
and this is a system that is trying

274
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:11,360
to incentivize the creation of intellectual goods.

275
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:15,780
So I think it's--remember, the spirit of the law is

276
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:20,800
to not just technically say no to everything,

277
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:24,860
but to incentivize the creation of intellectual goods.

278
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:27,620
And there's many forms of intellectual property

279
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:29,180
that are covered.

280
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:33,660
There's copyrights, patents, trademarks and secrets-- sorry,

281
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:36,620
trademarks, and trade secrets.

282
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:41,140
The thing that is most relevant for language model data

283
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:43,220
is copyright law.

284
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:46,980
So copyright law has a fairly long history going back

285
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:49,820
to the 1700s in England.

286
00:15:49,820 --> 00:15:53,460
And so 1709 was the first time that governments and courts

287
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:56,340
started actually regulating copyright

288
00:15:56,340 --> 00:16:01,740
for this goal of incentivizing innovation and creation.

289
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:05,580
And in the US, more recently, the Copyright Act of 1976

290
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:10,020
is what I think defines a lot of modern copyright.

291
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:13,620
So copyright protection applies to original works

292
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:18,580
of authorship fixed in any tangible medium of expression,

293
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:19,900
yada, yada, yada.

294
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:23,920
So a few notes.

295
00:16:23,920 --> 00:16:26,820
So not everything is copyrightable.

296
00:16:26,820 --> 00:16:31,220
In particular, collections are not copyrightable.

297
00:16:31,220 --> 00:16:36,900
So for example, if you have a telephone directory,

298
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:41,540
unless there is some creativity in how you arrange things,

299
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:45,220
this is not really copyrightable.

300
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:48,460
And furthermore, copyright applies to the expression, not

301
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:49,860
the idea.

302
00:16:49,860 --> 00:16:53,180
So you can't copyright an idea, like you can't copyright

303
00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:55,360
the quick-sort algorithm.

304
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:57,380
You can copyright the implementation

305
00:16:57,380 --> 00:17:01,740
of a quick-sort algorithm, but not the quicksort algorithm.

306
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:04,339
So one thing that happened in 1976

307
00:17:04,339 --> 00:17:10,359
is that the barrier to getting a copyright was relaxed a lot.

308
00:17:10,359 --> 00:17:11,640
So more things are copyright.

309
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:14,300
So it used to be that you had to publish something

310
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:15,760
to have something copyright.

311
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:19,260
Now things just have to be fixed.

312
00:17:19,260 --> 00:17:22,780
And what that means is that registration of any sort

313
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:26,388
is not required for copyright protection.

314
00:17:26,388 --> 00:17:27,680
This is in contrast to patents.

315
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:31,060
Patents you have to go pay a lot of money to get a patent,

316
00:17:31,060 --> 00:17:32,980
and you have to file a patent.

317
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:36,190
But the threshold for copyright is very low.

318
00:17:36,190 --> 00:17:38,470
For example, you put something on your website,

319
00:17:38,470 --> 00:17:40,550
it's copyrighted.

320
00:17:40,550 --> 00:17:42,670
That's it.

321
00:17:42,670 --> 00:17:45,990
Now if you want to sue someone, you

322
00:17:45,990 --> 00:17:50,670
have to go get it registered, but it only costs $65.

323
00:17:50,670 --> 00:17:53,310
Which is much smaller than the lawyer fees

324
00:17:53,310 --> 00:17:56,190
that you'll probably pay, so it's really

325
00:17:56,190 --> 00:17:59,670
still a very low bar.

326
00:17:59,670 --> 00:18:01,350
So one thing to know about copyright

327
00:18:01,350 --> 00:18:07,330
is that it lasts 75 years, and then the copyright expires.

328
00:18:07,330 --> 00:18:11,230
And whatever that content gets released into the public domain,

329
00:18:11,230 --> 00:18:15,550
and this is now--everyone can use it freely.

330
00:18:15,550 --> 00:18:21,070
So a lot of people who have lived in the past,

331
00:18:21,070 --> 00:18:23,250
all their great works are in the public domain,

332
00:18:23,250 --> 00:18:24,990
which is great for everyone.

333
00:18:24,990 --> 00:18:28,950
So the rationale here is that you're incentivizing innovation.

334
00:18:28,950 --> 00:18:33,870
So when an artist or a creator creates something,

335
00:18:33,870 --> 00:18:36,470
you want to protect their--

336
00:18:36,470 --> 00:18:38,590
you don't want someone else to just take it,

337
00:18:38,590 --> 00:18:40,010
so you protect them for a while.

338
00:18:40,010 --> 00:18:43,790
But after 75 years, presumably, it's not worth protecting,

339
00:18:43,790 --> 00:18:45,650
or they're passed away, or something.

340
00:18:45,650 --> 00:18:50,830
So it doesn't make sense to keep things copyrighted anymore.

341
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:57,670
So basically, everything on the internet is copyrighted.

342
00:18:57,670 --> 00:19:00,150
So you're saying, wait a minute.

343
00:19:00,150 --> 00:19:03,170
So that means is everything-- if I train on anything,

344
00:19:03,170 --> 00:19:04,730
that's a copyright violation?

345
00:19:04,730 --> 00:19:07,030
Well, not necessarily.

346
00:19:07,030 --> 00:19:09,350
So everything is copyrighted, but you

347
00:19:09,350 --> 00:19:10,850
can use copyrighted works.

348
00:19:10,850 --> 00:19:16,190
And the way you do it is A, you either get a license for it,

349
00:19:16,190 --> 00:19:20,750
or B, you appeal to the fair use clause.

350
00:19:20,750 --> 00:19:25,470
So a license from contract law is something

351
00:19:25,470 --> 00:19:28,050
that a licensor grants to a licensee.

352
00:19:28,050 --> 00:19:30,230
It basically says, don't sue me.

353
00:19:33,310 --> 00:19:38,790
You can use this work in the ways that the license permits.

354
00:19:38,790 --> 00:19:44,790
So there's a really nice license called Creative Commons,

355
00:19:44,790 --> 00:19:48,150
which allows something to act like it

356
00:19:48,150 --> 00:19:50,150
was in the public domain.

357
00:19:50,150 --> 00:19:52,570
It enables free distribution of copyrighted work.

358
00:19:52,570 --> 00:19:58,690
So examples include Wikipedia, Open CourseWare, Khan Academy,

359
00:19:58,690 --> 00:20:00,390
all these things.

360
00:20:00,390 --> 00:20:04,510
It was created in 2001 to essentially bridge

361
00:20:04,510 --> 00:20:06,330
public domain and existing copyright,

362
00:20:06,330 --> 00:20:09,790
because otherwise you have to wait 75 years.

363
00:20:09,790 --> 00:20:13,930
But what if the creator says, I actually want people to use it.

364
00:20:13,930 --> 00:20:16,810
They can now have a means, a legal means of saying,

365
00:20:16,810 --> 00:20:19,250
I put a Creative Commons license on it.

366
00:20:19,250 --> 00:20:22,430
It's going to act like it's in the public domain,

367
00:20:22,430 --> 00:20:27,430
and everyone can use it.

368
00:20:27,430 --> 00:20:29,590
So, so far, things in the public domain

369
00:20:29,590 --> 00:20:34,240
and things that have Creative Commons licenses are permissive.

370
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:37,040
You can use it.

371
00:20:37,040 --> 00:20:42,000
But there are many other times where

372
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,860
you don't have-- it's not a Creative Commons license,

373
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:47,540
it's not in the public domain, you have to get a license.

374
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:51,960
So if you have money, you can pay someone

375
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:53,140
to give you a license.

376
00:20:53,140 --> 00:20:55,920
So there's a bunch of deals between model developers

377
00:20:55,920 --> 00:21:01,040
and content, I guess, platforms, that

378
00:21:01,040 --> 00:21:04,600
allow the model developers to license the data for training

379
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:06,360
foundation models.

380
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:07,860
OK, so that's the first thing.

381
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:09,960
You can get a license-- either Creative Commons,

382
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:12,800
or you can pay someone for a license.

383
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:14,820
Now if you don't want to pay, you

384
00:21:14,820 --> 00:21:21,320
can appeal to fair use, which is a more complicated matter.

385
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:24,600
So this is section 107 of the Copyright Act.

386
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:30,080
And there are-- basically fair use says, I can use this anyway,

387
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:33,480
even if I don't have a license, and these four factors determine

388
00:21:33,480 --> 00:21:36,240
whether fair use applies.

389
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:40,300
So the first one is the purpose or character of the use.

390
00:21:40,300 --> 00:21:42,120
What are you trying to do with it?

391
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:45,980
And none of these are hard rules.

392
00:21:45,980 --> 00:21:48,200
They're just tendencies which have

393
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:51,040
to be weighed in court if it comes to that.

394
00:21:51,040 --> 00:21:53,120
So, for example, if you're trying to do something

395
00:21:53,120 --> 00:21:55,282
for education, it is more likely to be

396
00:21:55,282 --> 00:21:57,240
fair use than if you're trying to sell and make

397
00:21:57,240 --> 00:21:58,980
money off of it.

398
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,800
If you're trying to-- if you transform something,

399
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:03,520
then that's going to be more favored

400
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:07,800
than if you just literally host the identical copy.

401
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:12,080
The nature of the actual copyrighted work is also important.

402
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:14,240
So things that are more factual are

403
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:17,640
more likely to be fair use than fictional works.

404
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:21,840
Because if you write a summary of World War--

405
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:25,680
a page about facts about World War II,

406
00:22:25,680 --> 00:22:35,120
there's less protection over my really creative poem.

407
00:22:35,120 --> 00:22:39,820
And then the amount or portion of the original work.

408
00:22:39,820 --> 00:22:43,545
So using a snippet is favored over the whole work.

409
00:22:43,545 --> 00:22:44,920
So if you just take a little bit,

410
00:22:44,920 --> 00:22:46,740
that's not as bad as taking everything.

411
00:22:46,740 --> 00:22:48,480
So that's fairly straightforward.

412
00:22:48,480 --> 00:22:53,760
And the final thing is related to original motivation.

413
00:22:53,760 --> 00:22:54,960
It was like, why are you--

414
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:58,440
why copyright at all is to protect and align

415
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:00,080
the economic incentives.

416
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:03,720
So the effect that what the use has

417
00:23:03,720 --> 00:23:06,440
on the market for the original work.

418
00:23:06,440 --> 00:23:12,400
So if you take an author's work and you provide an alternative,

419
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:16,720
and that decreases the amount that the original author could

420
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:20,260
monetize their work, that is seen as potentially bad.

421
00:23:20,260 --> 00:23:24,480
But if you're transforming it in a way and going to a new market,

422
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:26,490
or doing something else with it, then that's

423
00:23:26,490 --> 00:23:30,250
going to be treated more favorably.

424
00:23:30,250 --> 00:23:32,710
So here are some examples of fair use.

425
00:23:32,710 --> 00:23:35,350
You can watch a movie and write a summary of it.

426
00:23:35,350 --> 00:23:40,290
You can re-implement an idea, like an algorithm--

427
00:23:40,290 --> 00:23:42,410
not copy the code.

428
00:23:42,410 --> 00:23:48,810
And then there was this famous lawsuit between Authors Guild

429
00:23:48,810 --> 00:23:50,650
and Google.

430
00:23:50,650 --> 00:23:52,610
So if you go to Google Books, you

431
00:23:52,610 --> 00:23:57,490
can see snippets of various books which are copyrighted.

432
00:23:57,490 --> 00:24:04,610
So there was a huge deal about whether this

433
00:24:04,610 --> 00:24:08,570
was deemed fair use, because you are redistributing

434
00:24:08,570 --> 00:24:14,890
other copyrighted work, and eventually--

435
00:24:14,890 --> 00:24:16,870
eventually meaning after 11 years--

436
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:19,890
this lawsuit was settled in favor of Google,

437
00:24:19,890 --> 00:24:23,010
which is why this thing exists.

438
00:24:23,010 --> 00:24:26,530
And this has set some precedent for thinking about

439
00:24:26,530 --> 00:24:33,290
whether language model training is fair use.

440
00:24:33,290 --> 00:24:36,170
One thing to note is that copyright is not

441
00:24:36,170 --> 00:24:39,050
about verbatim memorization.

442
00:24:39,050 --> 00:24:41,870
So this might be, I think, for an ML audience,

443
00:24:41,870 --> 00:24:44,890
this might be a little bit new because a lot of papers

444
00:24:44,890 --> 00:24:47,370
are focused on verbatim memorization.

445
00:24:47,370 --> 00:24:51,210
That's one way you can violate copyright, but not the only one.

446
00:24:51,210 --> 00:24:55,490
So for example, plots and characters can be copyrightable.

447
00:24:55,490 --> 00:24:57,090
So you can copyright Harry Potter

448
00:24:57,090 --> 00:25:02,770
the character, not any particular book, or anything.

449
00:25:02,770 --> 00:25:04,870
But there are some exceptions.

450
00:25:04,870 --> 00:25:08,090
For example, if you're parodying something--

451
00:25:08,090 --> 00:25:11,390
and so you're creating something that looks like a derivative,

452
00:25:11,390 --> 00:25:13,270
but as long as you're making fun of it,

453
00:25:13,270 --> 00:25:16,570
it's actually more likely to be fair use.

454
00:25:16,570 --> 00:25:18,450
So, yeah.

455
00:25:18,450 --> 00:25:20,710
So a lot of copyright is about semantics.

456
00:25:20,710 --> 00:25:23,170
It's definitely not about N-gram overlap,

457
00:25:23,170 --> 00:25:27,340
and also about the economics.

458
00:25:27,340 --> 00:25:30,660
So what is the implication for language models?

459
00:25:30,660 --> 00:25:36,340
So first off, copying data.

460
00:25:36,340 --> 00:25:40,660
Even if you're not training, even the mere fact of copying--

461
00:25:40,660 --> 00:25:43,180
which is in the word copyright--

462
00:25:43,180 --> 00:25:45,840
is potentially a violation already,

463
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:47,720
even if you don't do anything with it.

464
00:25:50,500 --> 00:25:53,340
Training a model--

465
00:25:53,340 --> 00:25:58,640
I guess this is not really a-- let's see, how do I take that?

466
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:03,540
This is not necessarily a fact, but intuitively

467
00:26:03,540 --> 00:26:05,920
has a transformative flavor.

468
00:26:05,920 --> 00:26:07,460
Because it's certainly not--

469
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:09,900
it certainly seems different than just

470
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:12,580
rehosting another work.

471
00:26:12,580 --> 00:26:17,420
It's doing something transformative.

472
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:21,740
And in some sense, the models are really

473
00:26:21,740 --> 00:26:25,080
trained on this data as a means to an end.

474
00:26:25,080 --> 00:26:26,960
You are trying to extract the general idea,

475
00:26:26,960 --> 00:26:29,560
learn about the world and how it works,

476
00:26:29,560 --> 00:26:34,940
rather than only just the concrete expression.

477
00:26:34,940 --> 00:26:39,200
And the other thing is that regardless of copyright,

478
00:26:39,200 --> 00:26:43,400
language models can definitely affect the market.

479
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:46,180
So I think this is also-- remember, that's

480
00:26:46,180 --> 00:26:49,500
the fourth item for fair use.

481
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:51,260
So by negatively affecting the market,

482
00:26:51,260 --> 00:26:54,860
you are more likely to be ruled not fair use.

483
00:26:58,160 --> 00:27:03,540
So fair use is a little bit slippery.

484
00:27:03,540 --> 00:27:07,340
Suppose you have fair use, or you have a license.

485
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:11,260
Remember that there's still terms of service that allow

486
00:27:11,260 --> 00:27:15,200
or prevent you from actually just getting a piece of work.

487
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:20,460
For example, YouTube has all these videos which are licensed,

488
00:27:20,460 --> 00:27:24,460
but the terms of service prohibits

489
00:27:24,460 --> 00:27:30,100
just downloading the videos using a bot, like scraping,

490
00:27:30,100 --> 00:27:31,280
which is another layer.

491
00:27:31,280 --> 00:27:35,900
So there's multiple layers of restrictions here.

492
00:27:35,900 --> 00:27:38,380
So let me talk a little bit about where things

493
00:27:38,380 --> 00:27:40,400
are on the legal landscape.

494
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:44,080
So back in 2023, New York Times filed suit against OpenAI,

495
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:47,520
saying you train on our news articles, and look,

496
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:48,240
here's evidence.

497
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:52,540
We were able to actually prompt ChatGPT to generate a news

498
00:27:52,540 --> 00:27:55,020
article almost verbatim.

499
00:27:55,020 --> 00:27:57,780
I don't think this is still pending.

500
00:27:57,780 --> 00:28:02,780
There's a case against Anthropic where the allegation was

501
00:28:02,780 --> 00:28:06,100
you pirated millions of books, and you trained on these books

502
00:28:06,100 --> 00:28:08,700
to make Claude.

503
00:28:08,700 --> 00:28:14,220
Just last year, this was a landmark ruling

504
00:28:14,220 --> 00:28:17,900
that said, actually, this particular instance of training

505
00:28:17,900 --> 00:28:20,670
is fair use.

506
00:28:20,670 --> 00:28:23,350
However, Anthropic, you're still in trouble

507
00:28:23,350 --> 00:28:29,310
because you pirated all these books, and that is a no-no.

508
00:28:29,310 --> 00:28:33,230
So notice that this has nothing to do with training, just

509
00:28:33,230 --> 00:28:35,650
the mere fact of pirating--

510
00:28:35,650 --> 00:28:43,110
I mean, which is not a new thing--is illegal.

511
00:28:43,110 --> 00:28:45,250
Anthropic actually, interestingly,

512
00:28:45,250 --> 00:28:48,790
had bought and scanned all the books,

513
00:28:48,790 --> 00:28:50,170
which is actually fair use.

514
00:28:50,170 --> 00:28:52,430
The Court said you're allowed to scan.

515
00:28:52,430 --> 00:28:54,690
You buy a bunch of books, rip off the bindings,

516
00:28:54,690 --> 00:28:56,730
scan it, and digitize it for your own use.

517
00:28:56,730 --> 00:28:58,030
That's fair use.

518
00:28:58,030 --> 00:29:02,610
But that doesn't absolve you of your sin of pirating.

519
00:29:02,610 --> 00:29:06,270
So you can't pirate and then buy it and say, actually, never

520
00:29:06,270 --> 00:29:10,030
mind what I just did first.

521
00:29:10,030 --> 00:29:12,750
所以结果是 Anthropic 支付了 15 亿美元

522
00:29:12,750 --> 00:29:14,330
与作者们达成和解。

523
00:29:14,330 --> 00:29:18,510
所以这大约是每本书 3000 美元。

524
00:29:18,510 --> 00:29:22,750
所以还有一起针对 Meta 的诉讼。

525
00:29:22,750 --> 00:29:25,830
指控是你们用我们的书进行了训练——

526
00:29:25,830 --> 00:29:29,170
抱歉——这正如我们后面会看到的，

527
00:29:29,170 --> 00:29:31,710
实际上在 Llama 论文中被披露了。

528
00:29:31,710 --> 00:29:36,250
而紧随 Anthropic 之后的判决是，是的，

529
00:29:36,250 --> 00:29:39,270
训练属于合理使用。

530
00:29:39,270 --> 00:29:41,650
然后他们还通过 BT 下载了一些书，

531
00:29:41,650 --> 00:29:42,890
所以那件事仍然悬而未决。

532
00:29:42,890 --> 00:29:47,050
所以那很可能——如果判例成立的话，

533
00:29:47,050 --> 00:29:52,350
这对 Meta 也不利。

534
00:29:52,350 --> 00:30:00,430
所以到目前为止的总结是，可以说，训练

535
00:30:00,430 --> 00:30:03,390
已被认定为合理使用，或者至少

536
00:30:03,390 --> 00:30:07,950
尚未被认定为非合理使用。

537
00:30:07,950 --> 00:30:10,610
这些裁决到目前为止范围都很窄。

538
00:30:10,610 --> 00:30:13,550
这并不是说对任何受版权保护

539
00:30:13,550 --> 00:30:19,030
内容的任何训练都属于合理使用，但在这些案例中，没问题。

540
00:30:19,030 --> 00:30:21,590
盗版书籍显然是违法的。

541
00:30:21,590 --> 00:30:23,190
我想我们早就知道这一点了。

542
00:30:23,190 --> 00:30:28,250
但这仍然是一个非常活跃且不断发展的领域。

543
00:30:31,070 --> 00:30:36,510
关于数据来源有什么问题吗？

544
00:30:36,510 --> 00:30:38,570
我想，在我们进入实际的数据集之前，

545
00:30:38,570 --> 00:30:43,830
我只想先用这个更

546
00:30:43,830 --> 00:30:46,530
宏观的社会背景来铺垫一下。

547
00:30:51,690 --> 00:30:52,190
是吗？

548
00:30:52,190 --> 00:30:55,530
[听不清]

549
00:30:56,030 --> 00:30:59,430
问题是，我怎么看待语音数据和 ElevenLabs？

550
00:30:59,430 --> 00:31:04,810
我对那方面了解不多，所以我们可以线下聊。

551
00:31:04,810 --> 00:31:05,310
是吗？

552
00:31:05,310 --> 00:31:07,510
[听不清]

553
00:31:07,510 --> 00:31:11,790
——因为我猜如果我拥有某项资产，我有理由

554
00:31:11,790 --> 00:31:14,190
去[听不清]。

555
00:31:14,190 --> 00:31:17,180
我是否可以——通常是 Creative Commons，

556
00:31:17,180 --> 00:31:21,960
但我能否之后更新我的许可来阻止某些使用？

557
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:24,320
那么，时间在这里扮演什么角色

558
00:31:24,320 --> 00:31:29,280
呢，比如我之后用更

559
00:31:29,280 --> 00:31:32,000
更宽松的许可来训练旧版本，如果创作者把它

560
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:33,918
改成更严格的许可？

561
00:31:33,918 --> 00:31:35,960
Yeah, so the question is, what happens with time?

562
00:31:35,960 --> 00:31:40,220
If you have one license and then it gets changed, what happens?

563
00:31:40,220 --> 00:31:42,840
So first, I'm not a lawyer.

564
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:46,840
But second, so this--

565
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:49,880
So the first license, I believe you're still

566
00:31:49,880 --> 00:31:51,820
allowed to train on it.

567
00:31:51,820 --> 00:31:54,840
But what happens is that the license usually

568
00:31:54,840 --> 00:31:58,227
applies to not just, let's say, a fixed set of documents--

569
00:31:58,227 --> 00:31:59,060
for example, Reddit.

570
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:01,800
There's constantly new content being created,

571
00:32:01,800 --> 00:32:03,680
and the license being changed means

572
00:32:03,680 --> 00:32:07,160
that you wouldn't be able to train on the later things.

573
00:32:12,240 --> 00:32:17,760
OK, so let's look at various sources of data.

574
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:20,880
So let's go back to crawling.

575
00:32:20,880 --> 00:32:25,360
So most model developers have their own crawler,

576
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:31,920
because they want to have full control over what the data is.

577
00:32:31,920 --> 00:32:33,640
Fortunately for the rest of us, if you

578
00:32:33,640 --> 00:32:37,160
don't want to build your own crawler,

579
00:32:37,160 --> 00:32:39,840
there's something called Common Crawl, which has been around

580
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:43,640
for quite some time, since 2007.

581
00:32:43,640 --> 00:32:47,610
And every month they run a web crawl, gets about three

582
00:32:47,610 --> 00:32:50,000
to five billion web pages.

583
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:53,300
Each crawl has some overlap with the previous crawl,

584
00:32:53,300 --> 00:32:56,360
but they try to diversify and get new pages.

585
00:32:56,360 --> 00:33:00,680
So there's 300 billion pages so far, which seems--

586
00:33:00,680 --> 00:33:01,852
this was from their website.

587
00:33:01,852 --> 00:33:03,560
It seems a little bit big, because if you

588
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:08,220
multiply this number by 20, you don't quite get 300 billion.

589
00:33:08,220 --> 00:33:11,280
But that's what they say.

590
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:14,150
So how many URLs are out there?

591
00:33:14,150 --> 00:33:16,070
That's really hard to estimate.

592
00:33:16,070 --> 00:33:21,650
So the Google search index is at least 100 petabytes,

593
00:33:21,650 --> 00:33:23,130
according to them.

594
00:33:23,130 --> 00:33:32,350
And each Common Crawl dump has about 2 billion web pages.

595
00:33:32,350 --> 00:33:35,490
So that's about 372TB.

596
00:33:35,490 --> 00:33:37,150
And this is not including images,

597
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:41,170
this is just mostly focused on the text.

598
00:33:41,170 --> 00:33:44,630
So crawling is conceptually straightforward,

599
00:33:44,630 --> 00:33:48,370
but all the gory details are in the implementation.

600
00:33:48,370 --> 00:33:53,190
You start with a bunch of URLs, and then you iterate.

601
00:33:53,190 --> 00:33:54,590
It's basically graph traversal.

602
00:33:54,590 --> 00:33:56,630
You pop a URL from a queue, you download it,

603
00:33:56,630 --> 00:33:59,390
and then you look at all the hyperlinks in that page,

604
00:33:59,390 --> 00:34:01,090
and then you add them to the queue.

605
00:34:01,090 --> 00:34:06,410
And generally this is done in parallel over many machines.

606
00:34:06,410 --> 00:34:10,130
And there's many decisions here--

607
00:34:10,130 --> 00:34:16,610
which pages to download, you have to respect robots.txt,

608
00:34:16,610 --> 00:34:19,210
don't overload the server.

609
00:34:19,210 --> 00:34:24,530
Sometimes the websites change, and so you

610
00:34:24,530 --> 00:34:30,489
want a policy that will download frequently changed pages,

611
00:34:30,489 --> 00:34:33,929
but not spend time downloading pages that don't change.

612
00:34:33,929 --> 00:34:35,830
So there's some policy around that.

613
00:34:35,830 --> 00:34:38,310
And I mentioned before, URLs are dynamic,

614
00:34:38,310 --> 00:34:43,370
so sometimes the same URL leads to different content

615
00:34:43,370 --> 00:34:46,290
depending on some state of the browser.

616
00:34:46,290 --> 00:34:49,949
But also, many URLs might lead to the same content,

617
00:34:49,949 --> 00:34:52,409
so there's a lot of duplication that

618
00:34:52,409 --> 00:34:55,290
happens if you're not careful.

619
00:34:55,290 --> 00:35:00,170
In particular, mirror sites explicitly is about duplication.

620
00:35:00,170 --> 00:35:02,770
So Common Crawl releases their dumps in two formats.

621
00:35:02,770 --> 00:35:04,810
One is a WARC file.

622
00:35:04,810 --> 00:35:07,950
So this is the raw HTTP response.

623
00:35:07,950 --> 00:35:10,530
So remember, HTTP protocol is that you

624
00:35:10,530 --> 00:35:20,470
send a get, here's a URL, and then out comes a HTTP response,

625
00:35:20,470 --> 00:35:25,170
and the WARC file is that response.

626
00:35:25,170 --> 00:35:26,790
They also do some processing.

627
00:35:26,790 --> 00:35:29,850
They convert to a WET file, which is necessarily

628
00:35:29,850 --> 00:35:32,490
a lossy process.

629
00:35:32,490 --> 00:35:35,730
It turns out that this is not necessarily the best way

630
00:35:35,730 --> 00:35:38,970
to use the web.

631
00:35:38,970 --> 00:35:42,370
The HTML to text, there's many tools

632
00:35:42,370 --> 00:35:45,610
for this, including trafilatura and resiliparse,

633
00:35:45,610 --> 00:35:48,870
and the way you convert actually does matter.

634
00:35:48,870 --> 00:35:51,610
So this is from the DataComp LLM paper, which

635
00:35:51,610 --> 00:35:53,070
we'll talk a little bit about.

636
00:35:53,070 --> 00:35:55,730
Data is an ablation, where you look at the web files

637
00:35:55,730 --> 00:36:01,450
that Common Crawl releases versus these other tools,

638
00:36:01,450 --> 00:36:06,310
and it seems like trafilatura and resiliparse are better.

639
00:36:08,900 --> 00:36:14,300
So one thing you can do is just rely on general web crawls.

640
00:36:14,300 --> 00:36:17,600
But often, the web is not a uniform place.

641
00:36:17,600 --> 00:36:20,260
It's not like there's a bunch of websites,

642
00:36:20,260 --> 00:36:22,920
and you just get some random fraction of them,

643
00:36:22,920 --> 00:36:25,020
and that's your dataset.

644
00:36:25,020 --> 00:36:28,540
There are specific pockets of really interesting, high quality

645
00:36:28,540 --> 00:36:30,700
content, and I'll talk about three of those--

646
00:36:30,700 --> 00:36:32,240
Wikipedia, GitHub, and arXiv.

647
00:36:32,240 --> 00:36:36,980
So Wikipedia, all of you know about.

648
00:36:36,980 --> 00:36:40,580
It's been around since 2001, and now there's

649
00:36:40,580 --> 00:36:46,220
67 million articles around all these different languages.

650
00:36:46,220 --> 00:36:53,380
And Wikipedia is not all content by any means.

651
00:36:53,380 --> 00:36:59,420
You can't have original thought in it,

652
00:36:59,420 --> 00:37:03,780
because everything has to be referenced and cited.

653
00:37:03,780 --> 00:37:06,700
So in some sense you could argue that Wikipedia

654
00:37:06,700 --> 00:37:12,200
doesn't contain anything that's not already on the web.

655
00:37:12,200 --> 00:37:14,820
Well actually, that's not true, because they can also

656
00:37:14,820 --> 00:37:18,580
cite books, which are-- obviously you can't easily get

657
00:37:18,580 --> 00:37:21,500
them, so, Wikipedia is so good.

658
00:37:21,500 --> 00:37:27,260
And also, there's articles based on notability,

659
00:37:27,260 --> 00:37:36,020
so not everyone can get a Wikipedia article about them.

660
00:37:36,020 --> 00:37:40,140
So anyone on the internet can write this content.

661
00:37:40,140 --> 00:37:45,040
This was the radical thing about wikis, and it's still a miracle,

662
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:48,140
I think, to me that this actually works.

663
00:37:48,140 --> 00:37:49,820
It just happens that any vandalism

664
00:37:49,820 --> 00:37:53,820
gets reverted by administrators, or bots, I guess, these days.

665
00:37:56,660 --> 00:37:59,580
There's a small number of Wikipedians-- as any peer

666
00:37:59,580 --> 00:38:01,460
production system, there's a small set

667
00:38:01,460 --> 00:38:04,420
of people who do most of the work.

668
00:38:04,420 --> 00:38:09,660
And for example, this guy has 5 million edits.

669
00:38:09,660 --> 00:38:12,680
And one thing about Wikipedia is that every few weeks,

670
00:38:12,680 --> 00:38:14,155
they do a periodic dump.

671
00:38:14,155 --> 00:38:15,780
So basically they take all of Wikipedia

672
00:38:15,780 --> 00:38:19,780
packaged into a nice TAR file, and then you

673
00:38:19,780 --> 00:38:21,580
can just download that.

674
00:38:21,580 --> 00:38:24,840
So this is important because you don't need to crawl Wikipedia.

675
00:38:24,840 --> 00:38:26,840
In fact, they don't want you to crawl Wikipedia.

676
00:38:26,840 --> 00:38:29,382
They just want you to download this if you're going to crawl,

677
00:38:29,382 --> 00:38:32,100
and this is much better than crawling.

678
00:38:32,100 --> 00:38:35,540
Just a fun aside, or good to know,

679
00:38:35,540 --> 00:38:40,980
is that there's this paper that says you can actually

680
00:38:40,980 --> 00:38:43,500
poison Wikipedia.

681
00:38:43,500 --> 00:38:47,560
So you might think that this vandalism gets reverted.

682
00:38:47,560 --> 00:38:50,340
So if you do-- attacker tries to do something fishy,

683
00:38:50,340 --> 00:38:52,140
you'll just get it rolled back.

684
00:38:52,140 --> 00:38:58,340
But what Carlini realized is that these periodic dumps

685
00:38:58,340 --> 00:39:00,820
happen at a regular cadence.

686
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:04,230
And so, what you can do is you can go in

687
00:39:04,230 --> 00:39:08,070
and you can just edit it right before the dump happens.

688
00:39:08,070 --> 00:39:12,810
And then the dump happens, and then the edits get rolled back,

689
00:39:12,810 --> 00:39:14,890
but the dump still has the malicious content.

690
00:39:17,790 --> 00:39:21,490
If you're able to inject things into web pages,

691
00:39:21,490 --> 00:39:23,590
that means you can cause--

692
00:39:23,590 --> 00:39:28,510
there's works that show that you can cause the model to, let's

693
00:39:28,510 --> 00:39:32,310
say, ascribe negative sentiment to any trigger phrase,

694
00:39:32,310 --> 00:39:34,190
like the iPhone.

695
00:39:34,190 --> 00:39:39,490
So one takeaway here is that if you consider adversaries,

696
00:39:39,490 --> 00:39:41,750
even so-called high quality contents might

697
00:39:41,750 --> 00:39:44,910
contain bad things in them.

698
00:39:44,910 --> 00:39:50,310
I think this has been fixed since then.

699
00:39:50,310 --> 00:39:53,690
OK, so GitHub is a good place for code,

700
00:39:53,690 --> 00:39:56,550
and code is important not just for

701
00:39:56,550 --> 00:39:59,410
if you want coding capabilities for your language model,

702
00:39:59,410 --> 00:40:03,630
but if you want general reasoning.

703
00:40:03,630 --> 00:40:06,670
So GitHub all of you know.

704
00:40:06,670 --> 00:40:10,430
Again, it's a live service for hosting code repositories.

705
00:40:10,430 --> 00:40:15,870
Been around since 2008, about the same age as Common Crawl.

706
00:40:15,870 --> 00:40:22,470
It has 420 million repositories, 28 million are public.

707
00:40:22,470 --> 00:40:26,790
Each repository contains-- it's not a file, as you know,

708
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:29,770
it's a directory with commit history, issues, pull requests,

709
00:40:29,770 --> 00:40:31,470
and comments.

710
00:40:31,470 --> 00:40:35,230
Code has generally a lot of duplicates,

711
00:40:35,230 --> 00:40:42,150
and it's just because either copying code, or forking code.

712
00:40:42,150 --> 00:40:46,430
And GitHub has deemed it-- you're

713
00:40:46,430 --> 00:40:49,390
allowed to train on any public repository

714
00:40:49,390 --> 00:40:52,430
with a permissive license.

715
00:40:52,430 --> 00:40:56,110
So MIT or Apache licenses are fine.

716
00:40:56,110 --> 00:40:58,345
So remember, there's two types of data

717
00:40:58,345 --> 00:40:59,470
when you talk about GitHub.

718
00:40:59,470 --> 00:41:01,880
There's the repository data, which

719
00:41:01,880 --> 00:41:07,160
you can just go and download through the GitHub Git protocol.

720
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:10,440
And again, you shouldn't scrape GitHub,

721
00:41:10,440 --> 00:41:12,640
you should just download the repository.

722
00:41:12,640 --> 00:41:16,120
And then the metadata associated with each repository-- issues

723
00:41:16,120 --> 00:41:17,700
and pull requests, and so on.

724
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:20,600
These are made actually via the GitHub Archive, which gives you

725
00:41:20,600 --> 00:41:23,720
hourly snapshots of this event stream, which I think

726
00:41:23,720 --> 00:41:25,380
is really interesting data.

727
00:41:25,380 --> 00:41:33,360
It's basically every single comment, or star, or action on GitHub that gets recorded.

728
00:41:33,360 --> 00:41:36,720
There's this Software Heritage Foundation

729
00:41:36,720 --> 00:41:41,440
that is focused on the repository, not the metadata,

730
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:44,218
and they aggregate GitHub.

731
00:41:44,218 --> 00:41:46,260
GitHub is not the only place where code shows up.

732
00:41:46,260 --> 00:41:50,540
There's also smaller entities like GitLab, Bitbucket,

733
00:41:50,540 --> 00:41:51,360
and so on.

734
00:41:51,360 --> 00:41:56,740
And they aggregate all that, those repositories

735
00:41:56,740 --> 00:42:01,280
So arXiv, just very briefly-- everyone knows arXiv.

736
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:04,360
This has been around as a site that allows people

737
00:42:04,360 --> 00:42:06,340
to share papers since 1991.

738
00:42:06,340 --> 00:42:12,560
Started with physics, and now has a lot of other areas in it.

739
00:42:12,560 --> 00:42:15,760
Each of the three million submissions

740
00:42:15,760 --> 00:42:21,640
has the metadata, a PDF, and optional LaTeX source.

741
00:42:21,640 --> 00:42:24,300
So already you should be thinking, well,

742
00:42:24,300 --> 00:42:26,040
what does it mean to train on arXiv?

743
00:42:26,040 --> 00:42:29,400
It's not clear, because this is, well,

744
00:42:29,400 --> 00:42:31,300
you have to convert the PDF into text,

745
00:42:31,300 --> 00:42:36,560
or you can use the LaTeX source, which is a bunch of files.

746
00:42:36,560 --> 00:42:39,800
It's not peer-reviewed, but there is some approval process,

747
00:42:39,800 --> 00:42:44,440
and authors can choose to maintain the rights,

748
00:42:44,440 --> 00:42:47,720
or put in the Creative Commons, which--

749
00:42:47,720 --> 00:42:51,440
so everything is actually very clearly licensed.

750
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:53,660
All the metadata is under permissive license

751
00:42:53,660 --> 00:42:56,240
so you can use that regardless, and then you can go

752
00:42:56,240 --> 00:42:58,120
and you look at all the papers that

753
00:42:58,120 --> 00:42:59,380
are Creative Commons license.

754
00:42:59,380 --> 00:43:01,480
You download those, and you can use them.

755
00:43:01,480 --> 00:43:08,160
And I think most arXiv papers are Creative Commons.

756
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:10,460
And then again, this is available.

757
00:43:10,460 --> 00:43:14,120
You don't crawl arXiv, you just go down to bulk

758
00:43:14,120 --> 00:43:18,640
download it from some website.

759
00:43:18,640 --> 00:43:22,500
So any questions about sources of data?

760
00:43:22,500 --> 00:43:23,300
Yeah?

761
00:43:23,300 --> 00:43:26,600
Are there any restrictions on usage of data,

762
00:43:26,600 --> 00:43:29,760
which is generated by models?

763
00:43:29,760 --> 00:43:31,720
Are there restrictions on data?

764
00:43:31,720 --> 00:43:34,140
Generated by models.

765
00:43:34,140 --> 00:43:34,640
Yeah.

766
00:43:34,640 --> 00:43:36,400
I mean, synthetic data models.

767
00:43:36,400 --> 00:43:37,760
Oh, I see.

768
00:43:37,760 --> 00:43:39,802
We'll talk about that a little bit later.

769
00:43:39,802 --> 00:43:41,760
So the question is, what about model-generated,

770
00:43:41,760 --> 00:43:43,100
like synthetic data?

771
00:43:43,100 --> 00:43:45,680
Are you allowed to use it?

772
00:43:45,680 --> 00:43:49,340
The short answer is probably yes.

773
00:43:49,340 --> 00:43:49,840
Yeah?

774
00:43:49,840 --> 00:43:52,760
When using crawlers like Common Crawl

775
00:43:52,760 --> 00:43:57,770
and stuff like that, I guess—because you mentioned earlier

776
00:43:57,770 --> 00:43:59,890
that there are websites that have

777
00:43:59,890 --> 00:44:02,130
a lot of pirated books on it.

778
00:44:02,130 --> 00:44:06,050
How do you ensure that those types of websites

779
00:44:06,050 --> 00:44:08,750
don't show up in your crawl?

780
00:44:08,750 --> 00:44:09,250
Yeah.

781
00:44:09,250 --> 00:44:11,290
So the question is, there's a lot

782
00:44:11,290 --> 00:44:12,710
of websites with pirated books.

783
00:44:12,710 --> 00:44:14,252
So if you're just doing a web crawl,

784
00:44:14,252 --> 00:44:15,710
you can't look at all the websites.

785
00:44:15,710 --> 00:44:18,010
So how do you make sure that everything is kosher?

786
00:44:18,010 --> 00:44:20,930
And that's part of the difficulty is, you can't.

787
00:44:20,930 --> 00:44:25,710
And so, there's—most likely in Common Crawl,

788
00:44:25,710 --> 00:44:30,090
there are copyrighted books and content that you are not

789
00:44:30,090 --> 00:44:35,110
supposed to train on, and you can—will appeal to fair use.

790
00:44:35,110 --> 00:44:36,527
Remember, everything is copyright.

791
00:44:36,527 --> 00:44:41,890
Books are not remarkably different from your website,

792
00:44:41,890 --> 00:44:44,670
for example. Both are copyrighted.

793
00:44:44,670 --> 00:44:48,680
Probably, a book author has more of a—

794
00:44:48,680 --> 00:44:50,330
if they want to go into court, they'll

795
00:44:50,330 --> 00:44:52,913
probably be able to protect that better because it's published

796
00:44:52,913 --> 00:44:58,410
than your website, but still.

797
00:44:58,410 --> 00:45:00,710
And I'll talk a little bit about later.

798
00:45:00,710 --> 00:45:02,610
If you really, really want to be careful,

799
00:45:02,610 --> 00:45:07,290
you can do something I'll show later.

800
00:45:07,290 --> 00:45:10,690
So what I'm going to do now is to now talk

801
00:45:10,690 --> 00:45:13,810
about the various datasets that are actually

802
00:45:13,810 --> 00:45:17,830
used in building models, starting all the way back to 2019.

803
00:45:17,830 --> 00:45:20,590
So we've talked about the origin of data and source of data,

804
00:45:20,590 --> 00:45:26,810
so you have a feeling for just the general data landscape.

805
00:45:26,810 --> 00:45:29,050
And so, let's start with BERT.

806
00:45:29,050 --> 00:45:34,730
So BERT was from 2018, and they trained-- back in the day,

807
00:45:34,730 --> 00:45:37,090
they just trained on Wikipedia and books.

808
00:45:37,090 --> 00:45:39,450
So what is this books?

809
00:45:39,450 --> 00:45:41,750
So there's this website called Smashwords.

810
00:45:41,750 --> 00:45:47,170
It was created that allow anyone to just put an ebook up

811
00:45:47,170 --> 00:45:53,050
to publish, and they had about half a million books in 2024.

812
00:45:53,050 --> 00:46:02,330
So in 2015, there was this paper who just scraped Smashwords--

813
00:46:02,330 --> 00:46:08,770
sorry, took the books that were free, and just made a corpus.

814
00:46:08,770 --> 00:46:10,290
This was back in the innocent days

815
00:46:10,290 --> 00:46:12,390
when no one was paying attention to any of this.

816
00:46:12,390 --> 00:46:15,530
So this books corpus was around for many years

817
00:46:15,530 --> 00:46:20,890
in the academic community.

818
00:46:20,890 --> 00:46:22,890
Since then, it has been taken down

819
00:46:22,890 --> 00:46:26,210
because it violated the terms of service.

820
00:46:26,210 --> 00:46:29,970
Just because it was free and you can get it, doesn't mean it

821
00:46:29,970 --> 00:46:33,890
was legally allowed.

822
00:46:33,890 --> 00:46:37,530
So one thing to note about Bert is

823
00:46:37,530 --> 00:46:43,153
that the sequences were documents rather than sentences.

824
00:46:43,153 --> 00:46:44,570
All the language modeling research

825
00:46:44,570 --> 00:46:50,210
before that was focused on sentences.

826
00:46:50,210 --> 00:46:54,000
So in 2019, GPT-2 came around.

827
00:46:54,000 --> 00:46:56,300
And so what did they do?

828
00:46:56,300 --> 00:47:02,020
So they wanted to get high quality web content.

829
00:47:02,020 --> 00:47:05,620
And at that time, people knew about Common Crawl,

830
00:47:05,620 --> 00:47:09,060
but somehow the Common Crawl data was too messy.

831
00:47:09,060 --> 00:47:11,500
So what they did was came up with this clever idea

832
00:47:11,500 --> 00:47:13,820
where you can take--

833
00:47:13,820 --> 00:47:17,900
you look at pages that are outgoing links from Reddit posts

834
00:47:17,900 --> 00:47:20,580
with greater than three karma.

835
00:47:20,580 --> 00:47:24,640
So a good post must link to good websites.

836
00:47:24,640 --> 00:47:30,940
So you grab those pages, and you get a 40 gigabytes

837
00:47:30,940 --> 00:47:34,100
of text, 8 million pages.

838
00:47:34,100 --> 00:47:36,820
They never released this data, but there

839
00:47:36,820 --> 00:47:39,620
was an open replication of webtext,

840
00:47:39,620 --> 00:47:42,320
which people used quite a bit.

841
00:47:47,700 --> 00:47:49,320
所以这就是 GPT-2。

842
00:47:49,320 --> 00:47:54,700
所以当你在学习这些内容时，我想你可以同时思考——

843
00:47:54,700 --> 00:47:58,000
有很多过滤和收集数据的方法，

844
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:03,480
观察这些设计选择很有意思，而且。

845
00:48:03,480 --> 00:48:08,340
所以 CCNET 是由当时的 Facebook 开发的。

846
00:48:08,340 --> 00:48:12,100
所以目标是创建大规模高质量的数据

847
00:48:12,100 --> 00:48:13,360
集用于预训练。

848
00:48:13,360 --> 00:48:14,860
他们特别感兴趣的是

849
00:48:14,860 --> 00:48:18,660
低资源语言。

850
00:48:18,660 --> 00:48:21,700
所以他们不想要某种非常手工化的流程

851
00:48:21,700 --> 00:48:25,020
只适用于英语。

852
00:48:25,020 --> 00:48:26,840
所以他们做了很多事情。

853
00:48:26,840 --> 00:48:32,340
他们做了去重，以及语言识别

854
00:48:32,340 --> 00:48:36,740
来训练一个分类器检测语言，

855
00:48:36,740 --> 00:48:42,420
只保留你正在寻找的语言。

856
00:48:42,420 --> 00:48:47,340
对于质量过滤，他们的想法是

857
00:48:47,340 --> 00:48:50,620
他们想保留看起来像维基百科的文档。

858
00:48:50,620 --> 00:48:54,780
所以维基百科当时被认为是有价值的、高质量的

859
00:48:54,780 --> 00:48:57,285
内容，他们使用了一个语言模型，

860
00:48:57,285 --> 00:48:58,660
该模型是在维基百科上训练的，你

861
00:48:58,660 --> 00:49:02,180
为一个新文档在该语言模型下打分得到概率

862
00:49:02,180 --> 00:49:06,300
来衡量它有多像维基百科。

863
00:49:06,300 --> 00:49:08,520
所以他们没有用指向 Reddit 的外链，

864
00:49:08,520 --> 00:49:11,740
而是在维基百科上使用语言模型。

865
00:49:11,740 --> 00:49:16,840
他们表明，因为你可以获得多得多的数据，

866
00:49:16,840 --> 00:49:22,340
你实际上可以超越仅在维基百科上训练的效果。

867
00:49:22,340 --> 00:49:25,300
这是一个之后会

868
00:49:25,300 --> 00:49:29,020
出现在其他一些论文中的工具。

869
00:49:29,020 --> 00:49:32,880
所以这里是另一项工作。

870
00:49:32,880 --> 00:49:37,420
这是来自 Google 2019 年的工作。

871
00:49:37,420 --> 00:49:39,940
所以这被称为 C4。

872
00:49:39,940 --> 00:49:45,780
这篇论文实际上更出名的是 T5 模型，

873
00:49:45,780 --> 00:49:47,670
它推动了这样一个理念：你应该把

874
00:49:47,670 --> 00:49:52,030
世界上所有的 NLP 任务都视为文本到文本。

875
00:49:52,030 --> 00:49:54,250
但实际上，这篇论文的一大贡献，

876
00:49:54,250 --> 00:49:58,030
这是一份非常长的报告，就是 C4 数据集。

877
00:49:58,030 --> 00:50:03,230
以及这个观察，在当时非常清楚，

878
00:50:03,230 --> 00:50:08,230
就是 Common Crawl 对于自然语言来说大多没什么用。

879
00:50:08,230 --> 00:50:12,030
当时有这样一种想法：你有这些小数据集，

880
00:50:12,030 --> 00:50:14,510
它们质量很高，或者你有

881
00:50:14,510 --> 00:50:17,030
Common Crawl 一团糟。

882
00:50:17,030 --> 00:50:19,390
任何试图在 Common Crawl 上训练的做法，都只会带来

883
00:50:19,390 --> 00:50:20,295
垃圾结果。

884
00:50:20,295 --> 00:50:21,670
所以人们在想，怎么

885
00:50:21,670 --> 00:50:26,110
才能从 Common Crawl 中获取更大但高质量的子集？

886
00:50:26,110 --> 00:50:32,670
于是我们看到了 Reddit 的思路，也看到了类似 Wikipedia 的语言模型思路。

887
00:50:34,670 --> 00:50:41,430
C4 用了不同的思路，它说，我们干脆定义一堆规则，结果这些规则

888
00:50:41,430 --> 00:50:42,890
相当有效。

889
00:50:42,890 --> 00:50:46,950
基本上，他们保留

890
00:50:46,950 --> 00:50:49,850
以标点结尾、且包含五个以上单词的行，

891
00:50:49,850 --> 00:50:53,150
删除少于三个句子的页面，

892
00:50:53,150 --> 00:50:57,510
删除包含任何脏词的页面。

893
00:50:57,510 --> 00:51:02,550
删除诸如使用条款、样板文字之类的内容。

894
00:51:02,550 --> 00:51:04,690
有趣的是，删除花括号，

895
00:51:04,690 --> 00:51:08,930
这过滤掉了很多代码——所以显然在当时，

896
00:51:08,930 --> 00:51:11,830
他们并没有考虑代码模型——

897
00:51:11,830 --> 00:51:17,270
并且他们过滤掉了所有非英语内容。

898
00:51:17,270 --> 00:51:21,730
最终结果是，你有 1560 亿个 token，

899
00:51:21,730 --> 00:51:25,090
也就是 800GB 文本。

900
00:51:25,090 --> 00:51:27,910
所以这比 GPT-2 数据集大得多，后者

901
00:51:27,910 --> 00:51:32,710
只有 40GB 文本。

902
00:51:32,710 --> 00:51:36,870
稍后有人在 C4 上做了一些分析，结果显示——

903
00:51:36,870 --> 00:51:39,170
这里是当时所涵盖的网站。

904
00:51:39,170 --> 00:51:41,670
所以你有一堆 Wikipedia，

905
00:51:41,670 --> 00:51:46,800
专利也是很常见的内容。

906
00:51:46,800 --> 00:51:49,340
他们还发布了一个类似 WebText 的文档。

907
00:51:49,340 --> 00:51:53,200
他们还使用了 Reddit 帖子中链接的思路——

908
00:51:56,880 --> 00:51:59,920
来自 karma 大于三的 Reddit 帖子，

909
00:51:59,920 --> 00:52:04,160
并过滤到那些页面。

910
00:52:04,160 --> 00:52:08,520
所以他们用这种方法能够获得，

911
00:52:08,520 --> 00:52:14,280
使用 12 个 Common Crawl 转储得到 17GB 文本。

912
00:52:14,280 --> 00:52:16,960
记住，WebText 是 40GB，

913
00:52:16,960 --> 00:52:22,960
这意味着 Common Crawl 可能是不完整的，或者并不

914
00:52:22,960 --> 00:52:25,160
包含所有内容，因为如果它包含所有内容，

915
00:52:25,160 --> 00:52:28,320
你应该能达到 40GB。

916
00:52:28,320 --> 00:52:30,380
他们展示了在当时，

917
00:52:30,380 --> 00:52:31,960
你可以用这些数据来提升

918
00:52:31,960 --> 00:52:36,480
一系列 NLP 基准的表现。

919
00:52:36,480 --> 00:52:40,360
所以我们看到了使用规则，这些东西

920
00:52:40,360 --> 00:52:44,000
that look like Wikipedia, Reddit links, and now let's

921
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:46,240
talk about GPT-3.

922
00:52:46,240 --> 00:52:50,280
So GPT-3, the dataset was--

923
00:52:50,280 --> 00:52:53,280
they used Common Crawl, they did their own processing.

924
00:52:53,280 --> 00:52:57,160
There is this WebText, which was essentially the GPT-2 dataset,

925
00:52:57,160 --> 00:52:59,160
but expanded.

926
00:52:59,160 --> 00:53:00,580
Notice that there's some overlap.

927
00:53:00,580 --> 00:53:02,600
Common Crawl does overlap with this,

928
00:53:02,600 --> 00:53:08,120
but this is, in some sense, a more targeted distribution.

929
00:53:08,120 --> 00:53:10,560
There is this-- in the paper, it's

930
00:53:10,560 --> 00:53:16,400
described as Books1 and Books2, which is internet-based books

931
00:53:16,400 --> 00:53:21,840
corpora, so this remains a mystery exactly what it is,

932
00:53:21,840 --> 00:53:24,560
and Wikipedia.

933
00:53:24,560 --> 00:53:30,460
So the result was about 500 gigabytes

934
00:53:30,460 --> 00:53:34,720
of text, 400 billion tokens.

935
00:53:34,720 --> 00:53:38,200
And to do the Common Crawl processing,

936
00:53:38,200 --> 00:53:41,640
they trained a quality classifier to distinguish things

937
00:53:41,640 --> 00:53:45,400
they deemed to be high quality from the rest,

938
00:53:45,400 --> 00:53:47,840
and they did some fuzzy deduplication

939
00:53:47,840 --> 00:53:54,720
because WebText and Common Crawl had dupes.

940
00:53:54,720 --> 00:54:01,360
So this paper followed the idea of using a classifier

941
00:54:01,360 --> 00:54:05,720
to do quality classification.

942
00:54:05,720 --> 00:54:09,060
So after GPT-3 came out, this was, I think, a big event.

943
00:54:09,060 --> 00:54:10,800
And there were a number of efforts

944
00:54:10,800 --> 00:54:12,500
to try to do things in the open.

945
00:54:12,500 --> 00:54:14,960
So one of the initial efforts was

946
00:54:14,960 --> 00:54:23,080
The Pile, which is a grassroots effort from EleutherAI, where

947
00:54:23,080 --> 00:54:29,800
they had a bunch of people in this Discord just jamming

948
00:54:29,800 --> 00:54:31,700
and figuring out high quality sources.

949
00:54:31,700 --> 00:54:34,480
And they came up with this list in the end.

950
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:38,140
And today, this is still pretty interesting and diverse.

951
00:54:38,140 --> 00:54:43,170
It has Common Crawl, it has PubMed.

952
00:54:43,170 --> 00:54:47,130
This has this thing called Books3, which we'll talk about.

953
00:54:47,130 --> 00:54:51,450
ArXiv, GitHub, which we talked about.

954
00:54:51,450 --> 00:54:56,770
Wikipedia, IRC, another BooksCorpus, Philosophy Papers,

955
00:54:56,770 --> 00:54:57,670
and so on.

956
00:55:04,090 --> 00:55:06,990
They included this Enron Emails dataset.

957
00:55:06,990 --> 00:55:13,690
So Enron went bust in 2002 and all the emails were released,

958
00:55:13,690 --> 00:55:16,850
and this is one of the few email datasets we have,

959
00:55:16,850 --> 00:55:20,070
which is a weird distribution, I think, for email.

960
00:55:20,070 --> 00:55:23,050
But that's what you get.

961
00:55:23,050 --> 00:55:25,170
So Project Gutenberg was--

962
00:55:25,170 --> 00:55:28,490
So let's talk about books a little bit.

963
00:55:28,490 --> 00:55:34,730
So Project Gutenberg was started in 1971,

964
00:55:34,730 --> 00:55:39,890
and it only includes books that received copyright clearance.

965
00:55:39,890 --> 00:55:42,030
So mostly books in the public domain.

966
00:55:45,210 --> 00:55:48,310
There's this packaging of Gutenberg into PG-19,

967
00:55:48,310 --> 00:55:54,290
which is Gutenberg before 2019.

968
00:55:54,290 --> 00:55:57,250
Which is, I guess, most of them, because for them

969
00:55:57,250 --> 00:56:01,610
to be in public domain, you have to wait 75 years.

970
00:56:01,610 --> 00:56:08,970
So Books3, which is this interesting dataset that

971
00:56:08,970 --> 00:56:11,090
appeared in The Pile.

972
00:56:11,090 --> 00:56:18,610
So this is described actually as 200k books from a shadow library

973
00:56:18,610 --> 00:56:23,730
called Bibliotik that contains all books from your favorite

974
00:56:23,730 --> 00:56:25,530
authors.

975
00:56:25,530 --> 00:56:29,170
And at that time-- again, 2020, no one was paying attention.

976
00:56:29,170 --> 00:56:35,930
People used it to train models, and since then, it

977
00:56:35,930 --> 00:56:37,230
has been taken down.

978
00:56:37,230 --> 00:56:42,970
So you cannot use, or should not use Books3 anymore.

979
00:56:42,970 --> 00:56:45,370
We'll come back to Books3 again.

980
00:56:45,370 --> 00:56:53,150
So Stack Exchange you've probably used quite a bit,

981
00:56:53,150 --> 00:56:59,125
although I guess these days maybe less so because of AI.

982
00:56:59,125 --> 00:57:01,000
There's a bunch of user-contributed questions

983
00:57:01,000 --> 00:57:03,570
and answers, started in 2008.

984
00:57:03,570 --> 00:57:07,770
And one thing that is interesting about this dataset

985
00:57:07,770 --> 00:57:12,610
is that it's a Q&A format, and this is quite

986
00:57:12,610 --> 00:57:16,850
close to a real application.

987
00:57:16,850 --> 00:57:20,090
So normally, you think about pre-training data as here's

988
00:57:20,090 --> 00:57:23,250
Wikipedia articles, just like raw text.

989
00:57:23,250 --> 00:57:26,250
But some parts of the web are actually--

990
00:57:26,250 --> 00:57:29,650
look supervised, like what you would want

991
00:57:29,650 --> 00:57:31,610
to ask your language model.

992
00:57:31,610 --> 00:57:37,140
Which I think, maybe, helps the model

993
00:57:37,140 --> 00:57:40,740
learn certain types of question answering behaviors.

994
00:57:40,740 --> 00:57:43,320
So not everything is super magically emergent.

995
00:57:43,320 --> 00:57:46,120
It's like, well, this type of data does exist on the web.

996
00:57:48,860 --> 00:57:52,880
So there's also-- this data has metadata,

997
00:57:52,880 --> 00:57:54,340
which is like the number of votes

998
00:57:54,340 --> 00:57:56,380
which are useful for filtering.

999
00:57:56,380 --> 00:58:02,120
And again, this data is released in data dumps

1000
00:58:02,120 --> 00:58:05,900
that allows you to just download without crawling it.

1001
00:58:05,900 --> 00:58:08,860
So now we're in 2021.

1002
00:58:08,860 --> 00:58:11,700
There was another paper from DeepMind.

1003
00:58:11,700 --> 00:58:13,240
They trained a model called Gopher,

1004
00:58:13,240 --> 00:58:17,100
which was never released, and actually

1005
00:58:17,100 --> 00:58:20,932
was subsumed by Chinchilla.

1006
00:58:20,932 --> 00:58:22,640
But the description of the data, I think,

1007
00:58:22,640 --> 00:58:23,560
is actually really good.

1008
00:58:23,560 --> 00:58:24,620
You should read this paper, because I

1009
00:58:24,620 --> 00:58:26,500
think it's very thorough in the way

1010
00:58:26,500 --> 00:58:29,660
that they describe the data processing.

1011
00:58:29,660 --> 00:58:31,900
Well, except for the parts where they don't tell you

1012
00:58:31,900 --> 00:58:33,860
what's in the data.

1013
00:58:33,860 --> 00:58:38,660
So they created this massive web,

1014
00:58:38,660 --> 00:58:42,260
and they had C4, which remember, was the dataset

1015
00:58:42,260 --> 00:58:44,700
that we looked at before from Google.

1016
00:58:44,700 --> 00:58:46,840
They train on books, news, GitHub, and Wikipedia.

1017
00:58:46,840 --> 00:58:50,860
They don't say anything about how they got that.

1018
00:58:50,860 --> 00:58:57,940
So for the MassiveWeb, they kept English, deduped,

1019
00:58:57,940 --> 00:59:03,260
and their quality filtering was also based on manual rules.

1020
00:59:03,260 --> 00:59:07,260
And part of the reason for this is that they

1021
00:59:07,260 --> 00:59:08,760
had more control over that.

1022
00:59:08,760 --> 00:59:12,460
So I think, as you can see, there

1023
00:59:12,460 --> 00:59:20,860
is this division between people who wanted to use rules,

1024
00:59:20,860 --> 00:59:25,580
and people who wanted to use classifiers.

1025
00:59:25,580 --> 00:59:29,120
So they got, as the name suggests,

1026
00:59:29,120 --> 00:59:33,180
a massive amount of text, but their model

1027
00:59:33,180 --> 00:59:37,630
was only trained on a very small fraction of this dataset.

1028
00:59:40,630 --> 00:59:48,870
So in 2022 Llama 1 came out, and the dataset here-- this

1029
00:59:48,870 --> 00:59:54,150
was actually a very nice paper in that it detailed their data

1030
00:59:54,150 --> 00:59:54,970
processing.

1031
00:59:54,970 --> 00:59:59,070
So this is probably one of the last non-open,

1032
00:59:59,070 --> 01:00:01,750
fully open models that actually talk about their data

1033
01:00:01,750 --> 01:00:02,870
processing.

1034
01:00:02,870 --> 01:00:05,970
So they have Common Crawl processed with CCNET,

1035
01:00:05,970 --> 01:00:09,910
which you know about.

1036
01:00:09,910 --> 01:00:14,230
The classification, though, was whether the page

1037
01:00:14,230 --> 01:00:16,990
was a reference of Wikipedia, not whether it is actually

1038
01:00:16,990 --> 01:00:19,830
a Wikipedia article.

1039
01:00:19,830 --> 01:00:22,550
So the idea is that well, maybe Wikipedia articles

1040
01:00:22,550 --> 01:00:24,950
are too stylized.

1041
01:00:24,950 --> 01:00:26,990
And we know that Wikipedia articles references

1042
01:00:26,990 --> 01:00:29,250
a bunch of other articles which are presumably good,

1043
01:00:29,250 --> 01:00:32,990
so let's call those the good websites.

1044
01:00:32,990 --> 01:00:37,870
They had C4, they did a bunch of processing

1045
01:00:37,870 --> 01:00:42,670
on GitHub to keep permissive licenses, Wikipedia.

1046
01:00:42,670 --> 01:00:47,910
They trained on Books3 and Project Gutenberg.

1047
01:00:47,910 --> 01:00:50,950
So Books3 really got them in a lot of trouble

1048
01:00:50,950 --> 01:00:53,030
because they're just announcing to the world that

1049
01:00:53,030 --> 01:00:54,450
hey, I trained on this dataset.

1050
01:00:54,450 --> 01:00:55,908
And if you look back, oh, this came

1051
01:00:55,908 --> 01:00:57,158
from-- where did it come from?

1052
01:00:57,158 --> 01:00:58,130
It came from The Pile.

1053
01:00:58,130 --> 01:01:00,010
Oh, it came from a shadow library.

1054
01:01:00,010 --> 01:01:04,430
So that's why people don't want to talk about their data

1055
01:01:04,430 --> 01:01:06,510
anymore.

1056
01:01:06,510 --> 01:01:11,750
So they looked at arXiv, and actually used

1057
01:01:11,750 --> 01:01:15,490
the LaTeX to do the processing, Stack Exchange,

1058
01:01:15,490 --> 01:01:17,740
and they got 1.2 trillion tokens.

1059
01:01:17,740 --> 01:01:19,490
They didn't actually release this dataset,

1060
01:01:19,490 --> 01:01:22,870
but they had enough of a description in their processing

1061
01:01:22,870 --> 01:01:27,910
that this was reproduced by Together's RedPajama V1

1062
01:01:27,910 --> 01:01:33,030
dataset, which was then used in other sources.

1063
01:01:33,030 --> 01:01:36,230
So as you can see here, RedPajama V1

1064
01:01:36,230 --> 01:01:43,790
initially also contained Books3, which has now been stripped out.

1065
01:01:43,790 --> 01:01:48,110
So various decisions made early on about copyright actually

1066
01:01:48,110 --> 01:01:53,350
influence-- have a fairly big watershed.

1067
01:01:53,350 --> 01:01:55,890
So let's talk about RefinedWeb.

1068
01:01:55,890 --> 01:02:03,630
So this is a paper that tried to make a point that web data is

1069
01:02:03,630 --> 01:02:05,130
all you need.

1070
01:02:05,130 --> 01:02:07,593
So the web, in some sense, is everything.

1071
01:02:07,593 --> 01:02:09,510
And then we talked about all these specialized

1072
01:02:09,510 --> 01:02:14,230
sources, like there's GitHub and arXiv, and Stack Exchange.

1073
01:02:14,230 --> 01:02:16,030
And they said, well, what happens

1074
01:02:16,030 --> 01:02:19,710
if we just stick with the web?

1075
01:02:19,710 --> 01:02:23,630
So they did a good job of doing the transformation from HTML

1076
01:02:23,630 --> 01:02:24,750
to text.

1077
01:02:24,750 --> 01:02:30,320
They filtered using the Gopher rules, which is basically keep

1078
01:02:30,320 --> 01:02:32,600
things that look like English.

1079
01:02:32,600 --> 01:02:34,480
They explicitly, at that time, still

1080
01:02:34,480 --> 01:02:38,600
said avoid ML-based filtering to avoid biases.

1081
01:02:38,600 --> 01:02:43,640
I don't want to find an overly narrow subset of the web.

1082
01:02:43,640 --> 01:02:47,700
They did some deduplication, and they had five trillion tokens.

1083
01:02:47,700 --> 01:02:51,960
They released about 600 billion of them.

1084
01:02:51,960 --> 01:02:58,900
So FineWeb from Hugging Face was a replication of RefinedWeb,

1085
01:02:58,900 --> 01:03:00,180
but improved it.

1086
01:03:00,180 --> 01:03:03,080
So they took all the Common Crawl dumps at that time,

1087
01:03:03,080 --> 01:03:05,240
did some filtering--

1088
01:03:05,240 --> 01:03:09,960
again, using manual rules to not inject biases--

1089
01:03:09,960 --> 01:03:16,060
deduped, did PII removal, and got 15 trillion tokens.

1090
01:03:16,060 --> 01:03:19,520
So you can see that the size of these datasets

1091
01:03:19,520 --> 01:03:22,020
is growing quite a bit.

1092
01:03:26,840 --> 01:03:31,320
So then there is this Dolma dataset from AI2,

1093
01:03:31,320 --> 01:03:36,560
and this is a dataset that includes their own processing

1094
01:03:36,560 --> 01:03:39,560
of Common Crawl, The Stack, which we'll talk about,

1095
01:03:39,560 --> 01:03:44,640
before C4, which you know about, and a bunch of other things.

1096
01:03:44,640 --> 01:03:53,200
And the Reddit dataset was from this project called PushShift.

1097
01:03:53,200 --> 01:03:55,760
And at that time, you could still get this data,

1098
01:03:55,760 --> 01:04:01,360
and you can train on it before things got locked down.

1099
01:04:01,360 --> 01:04:06,480
AI2 has their own crawl of academic papers called

1100
01:04:06,480 --> 01:04:10,400
Semantic Scholar, so they derived a dataset based on that.

1101
01:04:10,400 --> 01:04:13,960
So let's look at their Common Crawl processing.

1102
01:04:13,960 --> 01:04:19,180
So they use language identification.

1103
01:04:19,180 --> 01:04:23,360
So this was model-based, but quality filtering still

1104
01:04:23,360 --> 01:04:27,200
avoid model-based filtering.

1105
01:04:27,200 --> 01:04:34,280
And then they removed toxicity using rules and a classifier.

1106
01:04:34,280 --> 01:04:37,720
And they got three trillion tokens out of that.

1107
01:04:37,720 --> 01:04:40,360
So DCLM, I think, was maybe a point

1108
01:04:40,360 --> 01:04:45,160
where this idea of model-based quality filtering

1109
01:04:45,160 --> 01:04:51,040
started to really become the norm here.

1110
01:04:51,040 --> 01:04:54,280
So DataComp, the initial motivation

1111
01:04:54,280 --> 01:04:56,800
was to define some sort of pipeline

1112
01:04:56,800 --> 01:05:02,600
so that people can try different data methods in a standard way,

1113
01:05:02,600 --> 01:05:04,240
and show results.

1114
01:05:04,240 --> 01:05:06,600
But I think the main way that I think

1115
01:05:06,600 --> 01:05:10,280
people use this is just using their dataset that they released

1116
01:05:10,280 --> 01:05:14,200
to train models and do other things.

1117
01:05:14,200 --> 01:05:19,660
So they processed Common Crawl to produce DCLM-pool.

1118
01:05:19,660 --> 01:05:21,238
So this is completely unfiltered.

1119
01:05:21,238 --> 01:05:23,280
And if you look at this dataset, this is massive.

1120
01:05:23,280 --> 01:05:27,210
240 trillion tokens.

1121
01:05:27,210 --> 01:05:29,290
This is probably more than the number of tokens

1122
01:05:29,290 --> 01:05:31,190
that anyone really trains on.

1123
01:05:31,190 --> 01:05:34,190
But a lot of this is fairly low quality,

1124
01:05:34,190 --> 01:05:39,190
so then they had a pipeline where all that gets filtered.

1125
01:05:39,190 --> 01:05:42,330
If you only keep English, you have

1126
01:05:42,330 --> 01:05:45,030
some rules to really narrow it down.

1127
01:05:45,030 --> 01:05:49,130
You do dedupe, and then you do this model-based filtering,

1128
01:05:49,130 --> 01:05:52,630
and you end up with something that's 1.4%.

1129
01:05:52,630 --> 01:05:57,290
And this is the dataset that we'll see actually works

1130
01:05:57,290 --> 01:05:58,570
pretty well.

1131
01:05:58,570 --> 01:06:04,490
So the model-based filtering-- and this was strangely good.

1132
01:06:04,490 --> 01:06:11,970
So to train a classifier, they took OpenHermes,

1133
01:06:11,970 --> 01:06:15,170
which is essentially instruction data that

1134
01:06:15,170 --> 01:06:19,470
was generated by GPT-4, and ELI5,

1135
01:06:19,470 --> 01:06:23,790
which is a subreddit with various questions and answers.

1136
01:06:23,790 --> 01:06:28,370
So this is-- let's see.

1137
01:06:28,370 --> 01:06:32,110
These are the type of questions that are in ELI5.

1138
01:06:35,570 --> 01:06:39,330
So anyway, you get the idea.

1139
01:06:39,330 --> 01:06:44,910
So it's kind of weird, but somehow this works.

1140
01:06:44,910 --> 01:06:47,410
So the negative examples are anything from RefinedWeb, which

1141
01:06:47,410 --> 01:06:52,010
is, remember, it's just a very loosely filtered version

1142
01:06:52,010 --> 01:06:52,510
of the web.

1143
01:06:52,510 --> 01:06:56,570
It's basically the web, and it got 3.8 trillion tokens.

1144
01:06:56,570 --> 01:06:59,050
They train a fastText classifier-- think about it just

1145
01:06:59,050 --> 01:07:01,370
as a linear classifier.

1146
01:07:01,370 --> 01:07:06,090
And then they show that this magical classifier, quality

1147
01:07:06,090 --> 01:07:14,410
classifier, outperforms a bunch of other things that they tried.

1148
01:07:14,410 --> 01:07:16,210
So, DCLM.

1149
01:07:16,210 --> 01:07:20,230
This became, for a while, at least in the open community,

1150
01:07:20,230 --> 01:07:26,130
a bit of a gold standard for quality filtering.

1151
01:07:26,130 --> 01:07:28,270
Let's move on to Nemotron.

1152
01:07:28,270 --> 01:07:31,690
So Nemotron-- this is from NVIDIA--

1153
01:07:31,690 --> 01:07:35,510
they said that well, DCLM filters too aggressively.

1154
01:07:35,510 --> 01:07:39,250
It's removing most of the data.

1155
01:07:39,250 --> 01:07:48,090
And remember, they only got--how many-- 3.8 trillion tokens.

1156
01:07:48,090 --> 01:07:50,170
So we need more tokens.

1157
01:07:50,170 --> 01:07:51,410
So what do we do?

1158
01:07:51,410 --> 01:07:59,290
We are going to do something more, I guess, elaborate.

1159
01:07:59,290 --> 01:08:01,130
We're going to prompt our existing

1160
01:08:01,130 --> 01:08:07,287
用模型根据教育价值对 FineWeb 文档打分。

1161
01:08:07,287 --> 01:08:09,370
所以我们会提示一个语言模型，问：

1162
01:08:09,370 --> 01:08:11,170
这是否有教育价值？

1163
01:08:11,170 --> 01:08:14,410
去加一堆标注，训练一个 fastText 模型。

1164
01:08:14,410 --> 01:08:16,830
这就是一个分类器。

1165
01:08:16,830 --> 01:08:20,380
另一个分类器就是直接用 DCLM 分类器。

1166
01:08:20,380 --> 01:08:23,540
所以我们会用这些分类器。

1167
01:08:23,540 --> 01:08:26,479
我们还会使用合成数据。

1168
01:08:26,479 --> 01:08:31,183
所以这大概是其中之一——

1169
01:08:31,183 --> 01:08:33,100
我不知道是不是——它可能不是第一个，

1170
01:08:33,100 --> 01:08:36,779
但它是真正

1171
01:08:36,779 --> 01:08:41,380
倾向于用合成数据进行预训练的主要数据集之一。

1172
01:08:41,380 --> 01:08:43,580
所以对于低质量数据，

1173
01:08:43,580 --> 01:08:45,819
按照这些分类器的判定，

1174
01:08:45,819 --> 01:08:49,300
你用一个语言模型改写它，让它更——

1175
01:08:49,300 --> 01:08:51,460
看起来像 Wikipedia。

1176
01:08:51,460 --> 01:08:54,420
对于高质量数据，我们用一个语言模型

1177
01:08:54,420 --> 01:08:56,439
生成各种任务。

1178
01:08:56,439 --> 01:08:58,518
例如，给定一篇 Wikipedia 文章，

1179
01:08:58,518 --> 01:09:00,060
我可以生成问答，或者我

1180
01:09:00,060 --> 01:09:04,740
可以生成比如：请总结这份文档，

1181
01:09:04,740 --> 01:09:08,060
从中提取关键信息，等等。

1182
01:09:08,060 --> 01:09:14,140
所以他们构建出了一个包含 6 万亿 token 的数据集。

1183
01:09:14,140 --> 01:09:20,340
所以这比 DCLM 大得多。

1184
01:09:20,340 --> 01:09:25,899
作为参考，这在某种程度上

1185
01:09:25,899 --> 01:09:30,000
按某些标准来看仍然很小。

1186
01:09:30,000 --> 01:09:32,899
Llama 3 是在 15 万亿 token 上训练的，

1187
01:09:32,899 --> 01:09:35,340
Qwen3 是在 36 万亿 token 上训练的。

1188
01:09:35,340 --> 01:09:38,660
虽然有这个——

1189
01:09:38,660 --> 01:09:41,180
不清楚这些去重后的 token 有多大，

1190
01:09:41,180 --> 01:09:44,620
因为当你查看语言

1191
01:09:44,620 --> 01:09:47,200
建模论文中的 [INAUDIBLE] 计数时，其中一些是重复的。

1192
01:09:47,200 --> 01:09:49,420
比如如果你跑两个 epoch，那就是

1193
01:09:49,420 --> 01:09:53,340
token 数量的两倍，所以你看这些数字时

1194
01:09:53,340 --> 01:09:55,740
必须小心。

1195
01:09:55,740 --> 01:10:00,380
他们表明这个数据集更好。

1196
01:10:00,380 --> 01:10:07,760
他们的高质量子集超越了之前的数据集。

1197
01:10:07,760 --> 01:10:13,900
到目前为止，我们已经看到了各种不同的

1198
01:10:13,900 --> 01:10:15,920
过滤方法。

1199
01:10:15,920 --> 01:10:20,810
我觉得所有这些在某种程度上看起来都很相似。

1200
01:10:20,810 --> 01:10:23,190
你进行一次网络爬虫，你可以选择

1201
01:10:23,190 --> 01:10:24,610
我要用规则来过滤，

1202
01:10:24,610 --> 01:10:25,690
或者我要用模型。

1203
01:10:25,690 --> 01:10:27,148
如果我要用模型，那么我

1204
01:10:27,148 --> 01:10:29,390
必须判断什么样的数据看起来是好的，

1205
01:10:29,390 --> 01:10:31,910
然后尝试训练一个分类器，

1206
01:10:31,910 --> 01:10:35,130
并对我所有的文档进行分类，然后筛选。

1207
01:10:35,130 --> 01:10:36,710
似乎存在一种权衡：

1208
01:10:36,710 --> 01:10:39,170
你可以使用大量的Common Crawl——

1209
01:10:39,170 --> 01:10:44,650
如果你想要的话，可以得到240万亿个token，

1210
01:10:44,650 --> 01:10:47,310
但那可能会是非常低质量的数据。

1211
01:10:47,310 --> 01:10:49,650
或者你可以得到一万亿个token，

1212
01:10:49,650 --> 01:10:55,230
两者之间存在某个最佳平衡点。

1213
01:10:55,230 --> 01:11:00,990
让我谈谈最后两件事。

1214
01:11:00,990 --> 01:11:05,990
那么代码，回到许可这个问题。

1215
01:11:05,990 --> 01:11:09,270
The Stack是一个非常不错的项目，

1216
01:11:09,270 --> 01:11:14,150
它试图构建一个非常好的代码数据集，因为

1217
01:11:14,150 --> 01:11:17,150
到2022年已经很清楚，代码

1218
01:11:17,150 --> 01:11:19,550
将变得非常重要。

1219
01:11:19,550 --> 01:11:21,910
他们在最初版本中所做的是

1220
01:11:21,910 --> 01:11:27,310
克隆了137个仓库。

1221
01:11:27,310 --> 01:11:30,270
他们保留了那些采用宽松许可证的仓库，

1222
01:11:30,270 --> 01:11:36,530
移除了近似重复的内容，最终得到——抱歉，

1223
01:11:36,530 --> 01:11:38,630
3TB的代码。

1224
01:11:38,630 --> 01:11:42,390
然后在2024年，他们进行了更新。

1225
01:11:42,390 --> 01:11:46,490
在这里他们更多地利用了元数据，

1226
01:11:46,490 --> 01:11:50,370
比如GitHub上的issue、评论和PR，

1227
01:11:50,370 --> 01:11:52,630
以及来自Software Heritage的仓库，

1228
01:11:52,630 --> 01:11:54,230
我们之前讨论过这个。

1229
01:11:54,230 --> 01:11:57,750
他们还从各种网站抓取文档，

1230
01:11:57,750 --> 01:11:59,950
通过爬取这些网站。

1231
01:11:59,950 --> 01:12:01,690
他们做了大量处理。

1232
01:12:01,690 --> 01:12:05,732
在GitHub仓库中，有二进制文件，你可能

1233
01:12:05,732 --> 01:12:06,690
不想用它们来训练。

1234
01:12:06,690 --> 01:12:09,350
其中有恶意软件，你要把它们清除掉。

1235
01:12:09,350 --> 01:12:12,770
GitHub上很多内容，尤其是这些PR，是机器人，

1236
01:12:12,770 --> 01:12:14,270
所以你必须过滤掉这些。

1237
01:12:14,270 --> 01:12:17,670
去重，做PII脱敏。

1238
01:12:17,670 --> 01:12:20,810
有大量的pull request之类的东西，

1239
01:12:20,810 --> 01:12:24,590
他们基本上进行子采样以保持代表性。

1240
01:12:24,590 --> 01:12:27,110
and the dataset manageable.

1241
01:12:27,110 --> 01:12:30,730
They also did this thing, which was I thought was nice,

1242
01:12:30,730 --> 01:12:35,110
which is that there's many programming languages out there.

1243
01:12:35,110 --> 01:12:38,350
There's a lot of Python, a lot of C,

1244
01:12:38,350 --> 01:12:42,690
but low resource languages like Nim-- which I hadn't even heard

1245
01:12:42,690 --> 01:12:46,690
of-- they're not that common.

1246
01:12:46,690 --> 01:12:50,190
So what they do is they compile this code

1247
01:12:50,190 --> 01:12:52,410
into a low-level intermediate language,

1248
01:12:52,410 --> 01:12:56,730
LLVM, which everything, like C Compiler, can compile into this,

1249
01:12:56,730 --> 01:12:59,510
and they have juxtaposed the low resource

1250
01:12:59,510 --> 01:13:01,910
language and the intermediate representation.

1251
01:13:01,910 --> 01:13:05,390
So then the language model can actually

1252
01:13:05,390 --> 01:13:10,290
learn the mapping between the shared low level representation,

1253
01:13:10,290 --> 01:13:13,400
which has a lot of data on, and the thing that it does

1254
01:13:13,400 --> 01:13:16,160
has less data on.

1255
01:13:16,160 --> 01:13:21,880
And just for fun, you add in all the other good stuff

1256
01:13:21,880 --> 01:13:22,740
that you can.

1257
01:13:22,740 --> 01:13:26,200
So Stack V2 is mostly code, and then

1258
01:13:26,200 --> 01:13:32,960
plus other things that were used to train their coding models.

1259
01:13:32,960 --> 01:13:36,240
And one thing about pull requests

1260
01:13:36,240 --> 01:13:42,100
is that pull requests and all the metadata is not,

1261
01:13:42,100 --> 01:13:44,660
by its nature, a linearized sequence.

1262
01:13:44,660 --> 01:13:48,520
So there has to be some steps taken to linearize it.

1263
01:13:48,520 --> 01:13:50,440
So one important thing you have to decide

1264
01:13:50,440 --> 01:13:52,660
is how much context to provide.

1265
01:13:52,660 --> 01:13:55,240
So for example, an event might just be,

1266
01:13:55,240 --> 01:13:56,960
I change one line of code.

1267
01:13:56,960 --> 01:13:59,440
And that could just be one line, but presumably

1268
01:13:59,440 --> 01:14:03,520
to learn that, you want to provide some context, maybe

1269
01:14:03,520 --> 01:14:06,360
a few lines around it, or maybe the entire file surrounding

1270
01:14:06,360 --> 01:14:07,120
that diff.

1271
01:14:07,120 --> 01:14:08,600
And these are some design decisions

1272
01:14:08,600 --> 01:14:12,960
that you have to make for linearization.

1273
01:14:12,960 --> 01:14:16,240
And so at the end of the day, the tokens that they train on

1274
01:14:16,240 --> 01:14:18,320
look something like this, where it's

1275
01:14:18,320 --> 01:14:23,920
this XML-like structured data, where you have the PR,

1276
01:14:23,920 --> 01:14:26,360
and then you have a bunch of diffs.

1277
01:14:26,360 --> 01:14:29,400
And then for comments, you have basically the events

1278
01:14:29,400 --> 01:14:35,600
of a comment was posted, what the review state of that PR was

1279
01:14:35,600 --> 01:14:37,200
and so on and so forth.

1280
01:14:37,200 --> 01:14:39,640
So it's not just learning how to generate

1281
01:14:39,640 --> 01:14:44,800
code, but also the software development process around code.

1282
01:14:44,800 --> 01:14:49,760
So finally, I'll talk about the Common Pile.

1283
01:14:49,760 --> 01:14:52,640
So recall that almost all the data on the internet

1284
01:14:52,640 --> 01:14:55,160
is copyrighted.

1285
01:14:55,160 --> 01:14:57,160
Some of it is permissively licensed, some of it

1286
01:14:57,160 --> 01:14:59,920
is even in the public domain.

1287
01:14:59,920 --> 01:15:03,740
And while you can appeal to fair use and say,

1288
01:15:03,740 --> 01:15:08,920
I'm going to train on it anyway, this is not quite settled.

1289
01:15:08,920 --> 01:15:11,200
So if you're very, very risk-averse,

1290
01:15:11,200 --> 01:15:18,140
then you say, well, if I don't know whether it is OK,

1291
01:15:18,140 --> 01:15:19,200
that's a no.

1292
01:15:19,200 --> 01:15:23,240
If you take that attitude, then that's what Common Pile did.

1293
01:15:23,240 --> 01:15:27,760
So can you train a good model with only permissively licensed

1294
01:15:27,760 --> 01:15:28,380
data?

1295
01:15:30,960 --> 01:15:33,640
Or rather, how far can you get?

1296
01:15:33,640 --> 01:15:41,600
So this was a project that went and scoured the internet

1297
01:15:41,600 --> 01:15:44,160
for all the different types of data

1298
01:15:44,160 --> 01:15:47,740
that they could possibly find that was worth training on,

1299
01:15:47,740 --> 01:15:49,520
that was permissively licensed.

1300
01:15:49,520 --> 01:15:54,360
It includes Stack V2 in code.

1301
01:15:54,360 --> 01:15:56,560
It turns out that a lot of government proceedings

1302
01:15:56,560 --> 01:16:01,040
are actually permissively licensed,

1303
01:16:01,040 --> 01:16:08,690
wikis, some things on the web-- some news sites are actually

1304
01:16:08,690 --> 01:16:14,690
permissively licensed, academic papers, online forums,

1305
01:16:14,690 --> 01:16:18,670
things that are in the public domain, educational resources,

1306
01:16:18,670 --> 01:16:20,010
and so on.

1307
01:16:20,010 --> 01:16:22,210
So in the end, there were 8 terabytes

1308
01:16:22,210 --> 01:16:24,810
of data, which is actually pretty

1309
01:16:24,810 --> 01:16:29,450
good for permissively licensed data.

1310
01:16:29,450 --> 01:16:32,970
Doing this project is actually much harder

1311
01:16:32,970 --> 01:16:35,565
than maybe it sounds at first glance.

1312
01:16:35,565 --> 01:16:37,690
It's not just as simple oh, you look at the license

1313
01:16:37,690 --> 01:16:42,490
and it's like, oh yep, Apache, or CC, good.

1314
01:16:42,490 --> 01:16:48,050
There's some subtleties, versus license laundering.

1315
01:16:48,050 --> 01:16:51,350
So people are sloppy with licenses.

1316
01:16:51,350 --> 01:16:53,490
So people might take some copyrighted work

1317
01:16:53,490 --> 01:16:56,730
and just slap a CC BY on it.

1318
01:16:56,730 --> 01:16:58,430
Anyone can write this on the internet,

1319
01:16:58,430 --> 01:17:06,370
and it's hard to tell whether this is real or not.

1320
01:17:06,370 --> 01:17:10,150
还有一种常见的做法，比如，

1321
01:17:10,150 --> 01:17:14,510
Dolma是宽松许可的。

1322
01:17:14,510 --> 01:17:17,730
记住，Dolma是AI2的集合。

1323
01:17:17,730 --> 01:17:22,250
但集合许可证并不延伸到单个作品。

1324
01:17:22,250 --> 01:17:26,103
所以这个集合是——

1325
01:17:26,103 --> 01:17:27,770
我的意思是，首先，有一个问题

1326
01:17:27,770 --> 01:17:28,885
就是集合是否——

1327
01:17:28,885 --> 01:17:30,510
我认为集合可以受版权保护，

1328
01:17:30,510 --> 01:17:34,650
但我想你可以给任何东西加许可证。

1329
01:17:34,650 --> 01:17:41,270
但单个作品不一定没问题。

1330
01:17:41,270 --> 01:17:43,810
所以你不能只看Hugging Face上的数据集，

1331
01:17:43,810 --> 01:17:46,018
而且你——你在Hugging Face上看到的许多数据集，

1332
01:17:46,018 --> 01:17:47,310
它们有宽松许可证。

1333
01:17:47,310 --> 01:17:49,170
但如果你深入挖掘，实际上

1334
01:17:49,170 --> 01:17:52,850
在单个层面上并不是宽松许可的。

1335
01:17:52,850 --> 01:17:56,210
而且他们还决定放弃训练

1336
01:17:56,210 --> 01:18:00,570
任何合成数据，因为在未授权数据上训练语言模型

1337
01:18:00,570 --> 01:18:04,690
是不明确的。

1338
01:18:04,690 --> 01:18:12,740
所以可能没问题，因为从技术上讲，这些

1339
01:18:12,740 --> 01:18:15,460
是MIT许可证的开放权重模型。

1340
01:18:15,460 --> 01:18:19,340
你可以做任何事。

1341
01:18:19,340 --> 01:18:21,040
你可以随心所欲地使用它们，

1342
01:18:21,040 --> 01:18:25,900
但这些语言模型想必也是训练过的

1343
01:18:25,900 --> 01:18:27,120
在未授权数据上。

1344
01:18:27,120 --> 01:18:29,900
所以这有点像数据洗白

1345
01:18:29,900 --> 01:18:33,040
如果你真的诚实的话。

1346
01:18:36,100 --> 01:18:39,780
所以用这些数据，他们将其比较

1347
01:18:39,780 --> 01:18:46,980
与一堆其他模型，比如第一个Llama、MPT，

1348
01:18:46,980 --> 01:18:48,180
和Qwen。

1349
01:18:48,180 --> 01:18:52,800
在一堆基准测试上，他们表明这还不错。

1350
01:18:52,800 --> 01:18:58,360
我会说它肯定不如Qwen模型，

1351
01:18:58,360 --> 01:19:04,670
但它肯定胜过那些非常老的——

1352
01:19:04,670 --> 01:19:09,100
这是2023年的，我猜。

1353
01:19:09,100 --> 01:19:11,460
所以相当老的模型。

1354
01:19:11,460 --> 01:19:13,700
所以我会说，这里的结论

1355
01:19:13,700 --> 01:19:16,740
是你可以做得相当不错，但我

1356
01:19:16,740 --> 01:19:19,060
认为不做更多token仍然很难竞争

1357
01:19:19,060 --> 01:19:21,400
。

1358
01:19:21,400 --> 01:19:23,600
但我不认为这是最终定论，

1359
01:19:23,600 --> 01:19:26,220
而且我认为你可能可以从公开数据中榨取更多

1360
01:19:26,220 --> 01:19:29,900
permissive licenses if you try.

1361
01:19:29,900 --> 01:19:34,100
OK, so to summarize this lecture here.

1362
01:19:34,100 --> 01:19:38,640
So maybe hopefully now I've given you some appreciation

1363
01:19:38,640 --> 01:19:40,800
that data is a very rich topic.

1364
01:19:40,800 --> 01:19:42,980
It's not that data just falls from the sky,

1365
01:19:42,980 --> 01:19:47,740
or you just go on Hugging Face and you download a dataset.

1366
01:19:47,740 --> 01:19:49,920
Data has to come from somewhere.

1367
01:19:49,920 --> 01:19:53,460
And there's also a technical but also social context

1368
01:19:53,460 --> 01:19:59,540
in which data comes, because at the top,

1369
01:19:59,540 --> 01:20:03,420
the internet is a bunch of live services.

1370
01:20:03,420 --> 01:20:06,142
Someone has to do the work of producing raw data,

1371
01:20:06,142 --> 01:20:08,100
whether it be the website that gives you dumps,

1372
01:20:08,100 --> 01:20:11,580
or someone has to build a crawler, or something.

1373
01:20:11,580 --> 01:20:14,860
And then, someone has to make the decision of how to process

1374
01:20:14,860 --> 01:20:17,580
it into usable form--

1375
01:20:17,580 --> 01:20:21,100
filtering, transformation, deduplication, all these things

1376
01:20:21,100 --> 01:20:26,420
have an impact on the quality of your final language model.

1377
01:20:26,420 --> 01:20:28,580
And notice that filtering is probably one

1378
01:20:28,580 --> 01:20:31,000
of the most important things.

1379
01:20:31,000 --> 01:20:33,940
How do you go from 200 trillion tokens

1380
01:20:33,940 --> 01:20:38,080
to less than three trillion tokens?

1381
01:20:38,080 --> 01:20:42,340
I think that obviously is a huge reduction, which I think

1382
01:20:42,340 --> 01:20:45,420
merits a lot of attention.

1383
01:20:45,420 --> 01:20:48,500
Data is, in some sense, a key ingredient that

1384
01:20:48,500 --> 01:20:50,280
differentiates language models.

1385
01:20:50,280 --> 01:20:53,700
A lot of language models are roughly the same kind

1386
01:20:53,700 --> 01:20:58,420
of transformer architecture, but data is depending

1387
01:20:58,420 --> 01:21:00,600
on how you process it.

1388
01:21:00,600 --> 01:21:03,470
It can make a pretty big difference.

1389
01:21:03,470 --> 01:21:05,310
There's plenty of legal and ethical issues

1390
01:21:05,310 --> 01:21:10,070
around data, more than I can get into this lecture.

1391
01:21:10,070 --> 01:21:15,270
And also, this process is very messy.

1392
01:21:15,270 --> 01:21:19,110
Unlike some of the other parts of this class where things

1393
01:21:19,110 --> 01:21:24,310
are maybe more based on first principles,

1394
01:21:24,310 --> 01:21:28,110
data processing is, right now at least, a lot just

1395
01:21:28,110 --> 01:21:30,150
based on vibes you set up.

1396
01:21:30,150 --> 01:21:32,530
You define this classifier, you define this rule,

1397
01:21:32,530 --> 01:21:34,390
you set some threshold.

1398
01:21:34,390 --> 01:21:36,250
So there are many opportunities to improve.

1399
01:21:36,250 --> 01:21:38,510
So as you do your assignment four,

1400
01:21:38,510 --> 01:21:41,450
Maybe think about whether there are better ways to do this,

1401
01:21:41,450 --> 01:21:45,150
and this could be maybe a research direction.

1402
01:21:45,150 --> 01:21:47,090
OK, so that's it for today.

1403
01:21:47,090 --> 01:21:49,290
So next time I'll continue talking about data.

1404
01:21:49,290 --> 01:21:52,030
I'll talk a bit about post-training data,

1405
01:21:52,030 --> 01:21:56,080
and a bit more about filtering.
